// debts
// ── TERM DATA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Format: [number, term, pronunciation, pos, noun-def, noun-note-type, noun-note, verb-def (opt), verb-note-type (opt), verb-note (opt)]
const debtsTerms = [
  [1,"Installment Debt","/ɪnˈstɔːlmənt dɛt/","noun","A loan repaid through a fixed number of scheduled payments over a set period. Each payment reduces the principal balance by a predetermined amount, making the payoff timeline predictable from the start.","field","Installment debt has a finish line built into it. That visibility is one of its most valuable features — you know exactly when you'll be done."],
  [2,"Revolving Credit","/rɪˈvɒlvɪŋ ˈkrɛdɪt/","noun","A credit arrangement with a reusable limit that resets as balances are repaid. Unlike installment debt, there is no fixed repayment schedule — the borrower chooses how much to pay and when, within minimums.","course","Revolving credit is flexible by design, which makes it useful and risky in equal measure. The absence of a required payoff date is the feature that can become the problem."],
  [3,"Secured Debt","/sɪˈkjʊrd dɛt/","noun","Debt backed by collateral — a specific asset the lender can claim if the borrower defaults. Mortgages and auto loans are the most common examples. The collateral reduces the lender's risk, which typically lowers the borrower's interest rate.","field","Secured debt trades asset risk for rate advantage. Understanding what you've pledged, and what happens to it if payments stop, is essential before signing."],
  [4,"Unsecured Debt","/ʌnˈsɪkjʊrd dɛt/","noun","Debt not backed by collateral. The lender extends credit based on the borrower's creditworthiness alone. Credit cards and most personal loans fall here. Interest rates are typically higher to compensate for the greater lender risk.","course","Without collateral, the lender's only leverage is your credit history and legal recourse. That reality is priced into the rate you pay."],
  [5,"Collateral","/kəˈlætərəl/","noun","An asset pledged to a lender as security for a loan. If the borrower defaults, the lender has the legal right to seize and sell the collateral to recover the outstanding balance.","field","Collateral converts a promise to repay into a secured claim. Whatever you pledge, understand that the lender's interest in that asset is now senior to yours until the debt is retired.","To pledge an asset to a lender as security against a loan obligation. When a borrower collateralizes something, they are granting a conditional claim on that asset in exchange for access to credit.","course","To collateralize an asset is to stake it. The asset remains in your possession — but the lender's claim on it is real, recorded, and senior to yours until the debt is fully repaid."],
  [6,"Lien","/liːn/","noun","A legal claim against a specific asset, giving the lienholder the right to take possession or receive proceeds from the sale of that asset if a debt goes unpaid. Liens attach to property and travel with it.","course","A lien is not an abstraction — it is a recorded legal encumbrance. Before purchasing property, understanding what liens exist is as important as understanding the price.","To place a legal claim against an asset, securing a creditor's right to collect from that asset if the underlying debt is not paid. A lender, government, or contractor may lien property as a formal enforcement mechanism.","field","To lien a property is to attach a legal claim to it. The asset remains with the owner, but its transferability is encumbered — it cannot be sold or refinanced cleanly until the lien is satisfied or released."],
  [7,"Recourse vs Non-Recourse Debt","/ˈriːkɔːrs · nɒn-ˈriːkɔːrs dɛt/","noun","Recourse debt allows the lender to pursue the borrower's other assets beyond the collateral if the collateral sale doesn't cover the full balance. Non-recourse debt limits the lender's recovery to the collateral only — nothing more.","field","The distinction matters most when things go wrong. In a recourse state, a foreclosure can follow you. In a non-recourse arrangement, handing back the keys may end the obligation entirely."],
  [8,"Senior Debt","/ˈsiːnjər dɛt/","noun","Debt that holds the highest repayment priority in the event of a borrower's default or bankruptcy. Senior debtholders are paid before subordinated or junior creditors from whatever assets remain.","course","Priority matters when there isn't enough to go around. Senior debt is the first in line — that position commands lower risk and, typically, a lower interest rate."],
  [9,"Subordinated Debt","/səˈbɔːrdɪneɪtɪd dɛt/","noun","Debt that ranks below senior debt in the repayment hierarchy. If assets are liquidated to settle obligations, subordinated creditors receive what remains after senior claims are satisfied — which may be very little.","field","Subordinated debt carries greater risk by design, and that risk is reflected in higher yields. Knowing where your debt ranks in a capital structure tells you something important about your exposure."],
  [10,"Prepayment Penalty","/priːˈpeɪmənt ˈpɛnəlti/","noun","A fee charged by some lenders when a borrower pays off a loan earlier than scheduled. It compensates the lender for interest income lost when a loan is retired ahead of its intended term.","course","Read the loan agreement before making extra payments. A prepayment penalty can erode the financial benefit of paying ahead — especially in the early years of a mortgage or auto loan."],
  [11,"Acceleration Clause","/əkˌsɛləˈreɪʃən klɔːz/","noun","A loan provision allowing the lender to demand immediate full repayment of the outstanding balance upon a triggering event, most commonly a missed payment or default. The loan does not continue — it becomes due in its entirety.","field","Acceleration clauses are standard in most loan documents. Understanding that a single default can convert a long-term obligation into an immediate one changes how seriously any missed payment should be taken."],
  [12,"Negative Amortization","/ˈnɛɡətɪv ˌæmərtɪˈzeɪʃən/","noun","A condition in which a borrower's loan balance grows rather than shrinks over time, because payments are insufficient to cover the full interest accruing. The unpaid interest is added to the principal — the borrower owes more than they originally borrowed.","course","Negative amortization moves in the wrong direction. It is most common in certain adjustable-rate or minimum-payment loans. Recognizing it early is essential — the balance can climb well beyond the original loan amount before the problem becomes visible."],
  [13,"Balloon Payment","/bəˈluːn ˈpeɪmənt/","noun","A large lump-sum payment due at the end of a loan's term, after a period of smaller regular payments. The final payment 'balloons' because it represents the remaining principal balance in full.","field","Balloon payments create a cliff at the end of a loan. The regular payments may feel manageable — but if the balloon can't be paid or refinanced when due, the consequences can be severe."],
  [14,"Home Equity Loan","/hoʊm ˈɛkwɪti loʊn/","noun","A loan secured by the equity in a home, disbursed as a lump sum and repaid over a fixed term at a fixed interest rate. The home serves as collateral, and the borrower receives the full amount upfront.","course","A home equity loan converts the equity you've built into liquid capital — at the cost of securing new debt against your property. The fixed rate and fixed payment make it predictable, which has real planning value."],
  [15,"Underwater Mortgage","/ˌʌndərˈwɔːtər ˈmɔːrɡɪdʒ/","noun","A condition in which a homeowner owes more on their mortgage than the current market value of the home. Also called being 'upside down.' The homeowner's equity is negative — selling the home would not cover the outstanding debt.","field","Being underwater doesn't trigger immediate action, but it eliminates options. Refinancing becomes difficult, selling creates a shortfall, and the path forward narrows considerably until values recover or the balance is reduced."],
  [16,"Subprime Lending","/ˈsʌbpraɪm ˈlɛndɪŋ/","noun","The practice of extending credit to borrowers with lower credit scores or weaker credit histories, typically at higher interest rates to compensate for the increased default risk. Subprime loans are not inherently predatory but carry substantially greater cost.","course","Subprime lending serves a real need — access to credit for those who don't qualify for standard terms. The cost of that access, however, is significant and must be weighed carefully before accepting the terms."],
  [17,"Predatory Lending","/ˈprɛdətɔːri ˈlɛndɪŋ/","noun","Lending practices that exploit borrowers through deceptive terms, excessive fees, high interest rates, or structures designed to trap rather than serve the borrower. Predatory lending targets vulnerable populations and often results in a cycle of debt rather than a path out of it.","field","Predatory lending wears the surface of a solution while functioning as a trap. The markers: urgency, opacity, excessive cost, and terms that benefit the lender regardless of outcome. Recognizing them before signing is far better than discovering them after."],
  [18,"Payday Loans","/ˈpeɪdeɪ loʊnz/","noun","Short-term, high-interest loans typically due on the borrower's next payday. They require no credit check and are often the only option for borrowers with no credit access. Annual percentage rates can exceed several hundred percent.","course","Payday loans solve an immediate problem by creating a larger one. The fee structure converts a small, short-term need into a long-term obligation with extraordinary cost. They are a last resort, not a financial tool."],
  [19,"Title Loans","/ˈtaɪtəl loʊnz/","noun","Short-term loans secured by the borrower's vehicle title. The lender holds the title as collateral and can repossess the vehicle if the loan is not repaid on time. Interest rates are typically very high.","field","A title loan pledges transportation — often essential for employment — as collateral for a short-term cash need. The risk is disproportionate to the benefit in most circumstances."],
  [20,"Student Loan Forbearance","/ˈstuːdənt loʊn fɔːrˈbɛərəns/","noun","A temporary period during which a borrower is permitted to pause or reduce student loan payments, typically due to financial hardship. Interest may continue to accrue during forbearance, increasing the total amount owed.","course","Forbearance provides breathing room — but it is not forgiveness. The loan continues to grow during the pause, and the deferred payments plus accrued interest must eventually be addressed. It is a tool for managing timing, not eliminating obligation."],
  [21,"Income-Driven Repayment","/ˈɪnkʌm-ˈdrɪvən rɪˈpeɪmənt/","noun","A category of federal student loan repayment plans that cap monthly payments as a percentage of the borrower's discretionary income. If income is low enough, payments can be as low as zero. Remaining balances may be forgiven after 20 to 25 years of qualifying payments.","field","Income-driven repayment recalibrates student loan payments to reflect what a borrower can actually afford. The tradeoff: extended timelines, accruing interest, and tax implications if forgiveness eventually applies. The right plan depends entirely on the borrower's income trajectory and loan balance."],
  [22,"Loan Forgiveness Programs","/loʊn fərˈɡɪvnəs ˈproʊɡræmz/","noun","Programs that discharge remaining student loan balances after a borrower meets specific qualifying criteria — such as working in public service, teaching in low-income schools, or completing a required number of income-driven payments. Requirements vary significantly by program.","course","Loan forgiveness is real, but it is conditional and often misunderstood. Qualifying requires consistent compliance over years or decades. Knowing exactly which requirements apply — and tracking them carefully — is the difference between forgiveness and a missed opportunity."],
  [23,"Credit Utilization Ratio","/ˈkrɛdɪt ˌjuːtɪlaɪˈzeɪʃən ˈreɪʃioʊ/","noun","The percentage of available revolving credit currently in use. It is calculated by dividing total current balances by total available credit limits. It is one of the most influential factors in credit score calculation.","field","Credit utilization is the most immediately actionable element of a credit score. Keeping it below 30 percent — and ideally below 10 percent — can meaningfully improve a score within a single billing cycle. It responds quickly to both improvement and deterioration."],
  [24,"Hard Inquiry","/hɑːrd ˈɪnkwɪri/","noun","A credit check initiated by a lender or creditor when a consumer applies for credit. Hard inquiries appear on the credit report and can temporarily reduce the credit score. Multiple hard inquiries in a short window may be treated as a single inquiry for rate-shopping purposes.","course","A hard inquiry signals that you are actively seeking credit. Each one carries a small, temporary cost to the score. Understanding when they occur — and when they don't — helps manage the credit-building process intentionally."],
  [25,"Soft Inquiry","/sɒft ˈɪnkwɪri/","noun","A credit check that does not affect the credit score. Soft inquiries occur when someone checks their own credit, when an employer conducts a background check, or when a lender pre-screens for marketing purposes. They are visible on the report but carry no scoring impact.","field","Soft inquiries are informational, not evaluative. Checking your own credit as often as you like does no damage — a useful distinction to know when monitoring credit health regularly."],
  [26,"FICO Score Components","/ˈfaɪkoʊ skɔːr kəmˈpoʊnənts/","noun","The five weighted factors used to calculate a FICO credit score: payment history (35%), amounts owed (30%), length of credit history (15%), new credit (10%), and credit mix (10%). Each factor reflects a different dimension of credit behavior.","course","Knowing the weights gives you the leverage points. Payment history and utilization together account for 65 percent of the score — meaning consistent on-time payments and managed balances do more than any other actions to build and maintain strong credit."],
  [27,"VantageScore","/ˈvæntɪdʒskɔːr/","noun","A credit scoring model developed collaboratively by the three major credit bureaus as an alternative to FICO. Like FICO, it uses a 300–850 scale, but the underlying formulas and factor weights differ. Some lenders use VantageScore; others use FICO.","field","Most consumers have both a FICO score and a VantageScore, and they may differ — sometimes substantially. Knowing which model a lender uses gives a more accurate picture of how they'll evaluate your application."],
  [28,"Credit Freeze","/ˈkrɛdɪt friːz/","noun","A security measure that restricts access to a consumer's credit report, preventing new credit accounts from being opened in the consumer's name. A freeze must be lifted before applying for new credit. It is free to place and remove.","course","A credit freeze is one of the most effective tools available for preventing identity theft. It does not affect existing accounts or credit scores, and can be lifted temporarily when needed. It is a protective posture, not a permanent barrier.","To place a security restriction on one's own credit report, blocking lenders from accessing it and preventing new accounts from being opened. A consumer can freeze and unfreeze their report with each bureau independently.","field","To freeze your credit is to take active control of who can see your credit file. It costs nothing, can be reversed when needed, and is one of the most powerful self-protective moves available to any consumer."],
  [29,"Credit Lock","/ˈkrɛdɪt lɒk/","noun","A service offered by credit bureaus that allows consumers to quickly turn access to their credit report on or off through an app or online account. Similar in protective effect to a freeze but operated through a bureau's proprietary service rather than a federally mandated process.","field","Credit locks offer convenience; credit freezes offer federal legal protections. For most people the practical difference is minor, but the freeze is the more formally protected option under law.","To activate a bureau's proprietary on/off access control on one's credit report, typically through a mobile app or online account. Locking and unlocking happens more quickly than a formal freeze but operates outside federal statutory protections.","course","Locking your credit is the faster lever — convenient, responsive, and available through an app. It accomplishes much of what a freeze does, with less regulatory backing. For many people, the practical difference is small; for others, the legal distinction matters."],
  [30,"Authorized User","/ˈɔːθəraɪzd ˈjuːzər/","noun","A person added to another individual's credit card account who can use the card but bears no legal responsibility for repayment. The account's payment history and utilization are typically reported on the authorized user's credit report as well.","course","Being added as an authorized user on an account with a strong payment history is one of the fastest ways to establish or improve a credit profile. The legal responsibility remains with the primary accountholder — but the reporting benefit extends to both."],
  [31,"Cosign","/ˈkoʊsaɪn/","noun","A person who agrees to be equally responsible for repaying a loan alongside the primary borrower. If the primary borrower defaults, the cosigner is legally obligated to pay. The loan appears on both parties' credit reports.","field","Cosigning is not a favor — it is a full legal commitment. The cosigner's credit, assets, and financial standing are at risk if the primary borrower cannot or does not pay. It should be entered with the same seriousness as taking out the loan oneself.","To agree to be equally and legally responsible for another person's loan, accepting full repayment obligation if the primary borrower cannot or does not pay. The cosigner's credit is also affected by every payment — on time or late.","course","To cosign is to lend your creditworthiness to another person's application. It is not a technicality or a formality — it is a binding commitment. If they don't pay, you do. Your credit report reflects their behavior."],
  [32,"Guarantor","/ˌɡærənˈtɔːr/","noun","A person who agrees to repay a debt if the primary borrower defaults, but who is typically not responsible unless and until the borrower has failed to pay. The distinction from a cosigner is that a guarantor's obligation is secondary — it activates after the primary borrower's default.","course","A guarantor steps in when the borrower cannot. The arrangement provides the lender with a backstop and the borrower with access to credit they might not qualify for alone. The guarantor's exposure, though secondary, is very real."],
  [33,"Default","/dɪˈfɔːlt/","noun","The failure to meet the legal obligations of a loan agreement, most commonly by missing required payments. Default triggers a range of lender remedies depending on the loan type, including collection activity, credit damage, acceleration of the balance, or repossession and foreclosure.","field","Default is not a single moment — it is a status with escalating consequences. Understanding what constitutes default under a specific loan agreement, and what options exist before that threshold is crossed, is far more valuable than learning it after.","To fail to meet the legal repayment obligations of a loan — most commonly by missing required payments to the point that the lender's contractual remedies are triggered. A borrower who defaults has crossed a threshold with formal, documented consequences.","course","To default on a loan is to change your legal standing with the lender. What was a payment relationship becomes an enforcement situation. The options available before defaulting — forbearance, modification, negotiation — narrow significantly once that line is crossed."],
  [34,"Forbearance Agreement","/fɔːrˈbɛərəns əˈɡriːmənt/","noun","A formal arrangement between a borrower and lender to temporarily pause or reduce payments due to financial hardship, without triggering default. Unlike student loan forbearance programs, mortgage forbearance is typically negotiated directly with the servicer.","course","A forbearance agreement preserves the relationship between borrower and lender during a difficult period. The key distinction from default: it is proactive, documented, and agreed upon — a managed pause rather than a missed obligation."],
  [35,"Charge-Off","/ˈtʃɑːrdʒ-ɒf/","noun","An accounting designation used by a creditor when a debt is considered unlikely to be collected — typically after 180 days of non-payment. A charge-off removes the balance from the creditor's books as an asset but does not eliminate the debt. The borrower still legally owes it.","field","A charge-off is a creditor's bookkeeping action, not a debt cancellation. The account may be sold to a collection agency, and the debt remains collectible. On a credit report, a charge-off is a severe negative mark that persists for seven years."],
  [36,"Deficiency Judgment","/dɪˈfɪʃənsi ˈdʒʌdʒmənt/","noun","A court judgment obtained by a lender for the remaining balance owed after collateral is sold and the proceeds are insufficient to cover the full debt. Most common after foreclosure or vehicle repossession.","course","A deficiency judgment means the debt follows you beyond the loss of the asset. Even after a home is foreclosed or a car repossessed, the lender can pursue wages, bank accounts, or other assets for the shortfall — unless the loan is non-recourse or the judgment is discharged in bankruptcy."],
  [37,"Judgment Creditor","/ˈdʒʌdʒmənt ˈkrɛdɪtər/","noun","A creditor who has obtained a court judgment against a debtor, giving the creditor legal authority to collect the debt through enforcement mechanisms such as wage garnishment, bank account levies, or liens on property.","field","A judgment creditor has the legal system behind them. The transition from creditor to judgment creditor significantly expands the tools available for collection. At that point, the debt is no longer just a financial matter — it is an enforceable legal obligation."],
  [38,"Garnish","/ˈɡɑːrnɪʃ/","noun","A legal process allowing a creditor with a court judgment to collect a debt by requiring a third party — typically an employer or bank — to redirect a portion of the debtor's wages or funds directly to the creditor.","course","Wage garnishment arrives without the debtor's cooperation — it is directed at the employer. Federal law limits the amount that can be garnished, but the process is disruptive and public within the employment relationship. Addressing debts before a judgment is entered is always preferable.","To legally compel a third party — typically an employer or financial institution — to withhold a portion of a debtor's wages or account funds and remit them directly to a creditor with a court judgment. The process operates without the debtor's consent or cooperation.","field","To garnish wages is to collect through the employment relationship itself. The employer receives the order and complies — the debtor's paycheck arrives smaller. It is a visible, disruptive enforcement mechanism, and avoiding it requires addressing the judgment before it reaches this stage."],
  [39,"Statute of Limitations on Debt","/ˈstætʃuːt əv ˌlɪmɪˈteɪʃənz ɒn dɛt/","noun","The legally defined period during which a creditor can sue a borrower to collect a debt. Once expired, the creditor loses the right to pursue legal action — though the debt may still exist and can still be reported on the credit report for the applicable period.","field","The statute of limitations varies by debt type and state. A debt beyond the statute cannot be collected through a lawsuit — but making a payment or acknowledging the debt in writing can restart the clock in some states. Knowing the timeline is essential before taking any action on old debt."],
  [40,"Debt Consolidation","/dɛt ˌkɒnsəlɪˈdeɪʃən/","noun","The process of combining multiple debts into a single new loan or credit facility, typically to simplify repayment or obtain a lower interest rate. The underlying debts are paid off and replaced by one new obligation.","course","Debt consolidation can reduce complexity and cost — but it does not reduce the total amount owed. The behavioral discipline to avoid accumulating new debt after consolidating is where the real work lies.","To combine multiple outstanding debt obligations into a single new loan, replacing several payment streams with one. The goal is typically to reduce interest cost, simplify management, or extend the repayment timeline for lower monthly payments.","field","To consolidate debt is to reorganize — not eliminate. The balance follows you into the new loan. The advantage is structural: one rate, one payment, one servicer. The risk is behavioral: the cleared accounts can tempt new spending if the underlying habits don't change."],
  [41,"Debt Settlement","/dɛt ˈsɛtəlmənt/","noun","A negotiated agreement in which a creditor accepts a lump-sum payment for less than the full amount owed, in exchange for considering the debt satisfied. Settled debts are typically reported as 'settled' rather than 'paid in full' on the credit report.","field","Debt settlement resolves an obligation at a discount — but the credit impact is significant, and any forgiven amount may be taxable as income. It is a tool for specific circumstances, not a routine strategy, and carries meaningful tradeoffs.","To negotiate with a creditor to accept a lump-sum payment for less than the full balance owed, in exchange for releasing the borrower from the remaining obligation. Settling a debt resolves it at a discount — but with meaningful credit and tax consequences.","course","To settle a debt is to close a chapter at a negotiated price. The creditor accepts less than the full amount; the borrower accepts a credit mark and potential tax liability on the forgiven balance. It is a practical tool in specific circumstances — not a shortcut."],
  [42,"Credit Counseling","/ˈkrɛdɪt ˈkaʊnsəlɪŋ/","noun","A professional service that helps individuals manage debt, develop budgets, and navigate financial hardship. Credit counselors may negotiate with creditors on a client's behalf and can facilitate debt management plans that consolidate payments at reduced rates.","course","Credit counseling provides structure and negotiation leverage for people in debt difficulty. A reputable nonprofit credit counselor is a resource, not a product — the goal is resolution, not a new financial obligation. Not all credit counseling services are created equal; source matters."],
  [43,"Debt Forgiveness","/dɛt fərˈɡɪvnəs/","noun","The cancellation of all or part of a debt by the creditor, relieving the borrower of the obligation to repay the forgiven amount. Debt forgiveness is distinct from debt settlement — it may be unilateral on the creditor's part.","field","Debt forgiveness sounds final — and in terms of repayment obligation, it often is. But forgiven debt is frequently treated as taxable income by the IRS. The financial relief on one side can arrive with a tax obligation on the other.","To cancel all or part of a debt owed by a borrower, releasing them from the repayment obligation for the forgiven amount. A creditor who forgives debt typically does so unilaterally — the decision to forgive belongs to the lender, not the borrower.","course","To forgive a debt is to extinguish the obligation to repay it. For the borrower, the relief is real. But the IRS often views forgiven debt as income received — meaning the tax consequence can arrive in the same year the financial burden is lifted."],
  [44,"Cancellation of Debt Income","/ˌkænsəˈleɪʃən əv dɛt ˈɪnkʌm/","noun","Income recognized by the IRS when a debt is forgiven, canceled, or discharged for less than the full amount owed. The forgiven amount is generally treated as taxable ordinary income in the year it is canceled, unless an exclusion applies.","course","Canceled debt is not free money in the eyes of the IRS. When a creditor issues a 1099-C for a forgiven balance, that amount enters the borrower's taxable income. Planning for this in advance — or understanding available exclusions such as insolvency — is essential."],
  [45,"1099-C","/wʌn-oʊ-naɪn-naɪn siː/","noun","An IRS tax form issued by a creditor when $600 or more of debt has been canceled or forgiven. The borrower receives a copy and must generally report the forgiven amount as income on their tax return for that year.","field","A 1099-C arrives after the fact — often as a surprise. Receiving one signals that a canceled debt has been reported to the IRS and must be addressed on the tax return. Ignoring it creates a tax problem on top of what was already a debt problem."],
  [46,"Short Sale","/ʃɔːrt seɪl/","noun","A real estate transaction in which a homeowner sells a property for less than the outstanding mortgage balance, with the lender's approval. The lender agrees to accept the sale proceeds as full or partial satisfaction of the debt.","course","A short sale requires the lender's cooperation and produces a significant credit impact — but less severe than foreclosure. In a recourse state, the lender may still pursue a deficiency judgment for the shortfall unless specifically waived in the short sale agreement.","To sell a mortgaged property for less than the outstanding loan balance, with the lender's prior approval. The proceeds go directly to the lender, and the shortfall is either forgiven or remains as a deficiency — depending on the agreement reached.","field","To short-sell a property is to exit a position that the math no longer supports — with the lender's consent. The cooperation of the lender is not guaranteed; it must be negotiated. The alternative — foreclosure — is typically worse for both parties, which is the leverage the borrower brings to the table."],
  [47,"Deed in Lieu","/diːd ɪn ljuː/","noun","An arrangement in which a homeowner voluntarily transfers the property title to the lender to avoid foreclosure. In exchange, the lender typically agrees to release the borrower from the mortgage obligation.","field","A deed in lieu is a negotiated exit from an unsustainable mortgage. It avoids the foreclosure process — which is faster and less adversarial — but the credit impact is still substantial. The key negotiating point is whether the lender waives any deficiency claim."],
  [48,"Bankruptcy Chapter 7","/ˈbæŋkrʌptsi ˈtʃæptər ˈsɛvən/","noun","A federal bankruptcy proceeding that discharges most unsecured debts — credit cards, medical bills, personal loans — after the liquidation of non-exempt assets. It provides a relatively quick financial fresh start, typically resolved within three to six months.","course","Chapter 7 is designed for people with limited income and significant unsecured debt. The relief is real and swift, but the impact on credit is severe and lasting — seven to ten years on the credit report. It is a last resort, not a planning tool."],
  [49,"Bankruptcy Chapter 11","/ˈbæŋkrʌptsi ˈtʃæptər ɪˈlɛvən/","noun","A federal bankruptcy proceeding primarily used by businesses to restructure debts and operations while continuing to function. Individuals with very high debt levels may also file Chapter 11, though it is rare and complex.","field","Chapter 11 reorganizes rather than liquidates. It preserves the enterprise while creating a court-supervised repayment structure. For individuals, it is typically only relevant when debt levels exceed Chapter 13 limits — it is a business tool that occasionally applies to individuals in extraordinary circumstances."],
  [50,"Bankruptcy Chapter 13","/ˈbæŋkrʌptsi ˈtʃæptər ˈθɜːrtiːn/","noun","A federal bankruptcy proceeding that allows individuals with regular income to restructure and repay all or part of their debts over a three-to-five year plan, without liquidating assets. It is often used to save a home from foreclosure or manage secured debts that cannot be discharged.","course","Chapter 13 is a repayment plan, not a discharge. The distinction from Chapter 7 is significant: assets are protected, but the commitment is longer and the obligation remains real throughout the plan. For homeowners with equity or assets worth preserving, Chapter 13 is often the more appropriate path."],
  [51,"Qualified Opportunity Zone","/ˈkwɒlɪfaɪd ˌɒpərˈtjuːnɪti zoʊn/","noun","A designated economically distressed community where new investments may be eligible for preferential tax treatment. Investors who reinvest capital gains into Qualified Opportunity Funds — which deploy capital in these zones — may defer, reduce, or eliminate capital gains taxes depending on the holding period.","field","Opportunity zones convert capital gains from a tax liability into a potentially tax-advantaged investment vehicle. The mechanics are specific and the rules complex, but the core concept — redirecting gains into qualifying investments in exchange for tax benefits — appears with increasing frequency in planning conversations involving business sales, real estate, or significant investment portfolios."]
];

// volatility
const volatilityTerms = [
  [1,"Benchmark","/ˈbɛntʃmɑːrk/","noun","A standard against which investment performance is measured. Common benchmarks include the S&P 500 for U.S. large cap equities, the Bloomberg U.S. Aggregate Bond Index for investment grade bonds, and the 60/40 blended index for balanced portfolios. Benchmarks allow investors and advisors to evaluate whether a portfolio is performing in line with, above, or below the relevant market.","field","A benchmark is the answer to the question: compared to what? Without a relevant benchmark, a return number is nearly meaningless. A 10 percent return looks excellent in a year the market fell 20 percent and disappointing in a year it rose 25 percent. The benchmark provides the context that transforms a number into a verdict."],
  [2,"Variance","/ˈvɛəriəns/","noun","A statistical measure of the spread of returns around the average — calculated as the average of the squared differences between each return and the mean. Higher variance indicates that returns fluctuate more widely; lower variance indicates more consistent returns. Variance is the mathematical foundation from which standard deviation is derived.","course","Variance is the raw material of volatility measurement. It is expressed in squared units, which makes it useful mathematically but less intuitive practically. Standard deviation — the square root of variance — converts it back to the same units as the original returns, making it interpretable as a typical range of deviation from the average."],
  [3,"Standard Deviation","/ˈstændərd ˌdɛviˈeɪʃən/","noun","A measure of the dispersion of returns around the average, expressed in the same units as the returns themselves. In a normal distribution, approximately 68 percent of returns fall within one standard deviation of the mean, and 95 percent fall within two. Higher standard deviation means more volatile, less predictable returns.","field","Standard deviation is the most widely used measure of investment volatility. A portfolio with a 10 percent average annual return and 15 percent standard deviation is expected to produce returns between negative 5 percent and positive 25 percent in roughly two of every three years — and outside that range one year in three. Understanding this range helps set realistic expectations for the ride."],
  [4,"Downside Deviation","/ˈdaʊnsaɪd ˌdɛviˈeɪʃən/","noun","A variation of standard deviation that measures only the dispersion of returns that fall below a specified threshold — typically zero or the risk-free rate. It ignores upside variability on the grounds that surprising to the upside is not a risk investors need to manage.","course","Downside deviation is a more intuitive measure of investment risk than standard deviation for most investors. The concern is losing money, not gaining unexpectedly. A security that swings wildly upward but rarely falls would show high standard deviation but low downside deviation — and downside deviation correctly identifies it as less risky from a loss perspective."],
  [5,"Historical Volatility","/hɪˈstɔːrɪkəl ˌvɒləˈtɪlɪti/","noun","The actual volatility of a security or index over a past period, calculated from observed price movements. Expressed as an annualized standard deviation of returns. It tells you how volatile something has been; it does not predict how volatile it will be in the future.","field","Historical volatility is backward-looking by definition. It is useful for understanding what has happened and for calibrating expectations — but markets regularly produce volatility that exceeds any historical precedent. Using historical volatility as a ceiling on what can happen is one of the most common and consequential errors in risk assessment."],
  [6,"Implied Volatility","/ɪmˈplaɪd ˌvɒləˈtɪlɪti/","noun","A forward-looking measure of expected volatility derived from the market price of options contracts. When option prices are high, implied volatility is high — the market expects larger price swings. When option prices are low, implied volatility is low — the market expects calmer conditions. Implied volatility reflects collective market expectations, not historical reality.","course","Implied volatility is the market's consensus forecast of future turbulence. It is extracted from current option prices through mathematical models and represents what investors are willing to pay for protection or speculation. When implied volatility is much higher than historical volatility, the market is pricing in more uncertainty than recent history would suggest — a signal worth interpreting carefully."],
  [7,"VIX","/vɪks/","noun","The CBOE Volatility Index — often called the fear gauge. It measures the market's expectation of 30-day volatility in the S&P 500, derived from a range of options prices. Readings below 20 generally indicate calm market conditions; readings above 30 signal elevated fear; spikes above 40 accompany genuine market crises.","field","The VIX is not a prediction — it is a real-time read on market anxiety. It rises sharply when investors are buying protection and falls when they are calm or complacent. A high VIX does not guarantee a market decline; markets can remain volatile and still trend upward. But historically, very high VIX readings have coincided with points of maximum fear that preceded recovery — which is why contrarians pay close attention to it."],
  [8,"Correction","/kəˈrɛkʃən/","noun","A decline in the price of a security or index of 10 percent or more from a recent peak, without reaching the 20 percent threshold that defines a bear market. Corrections are common, occurring roughly once per year on average in U.S. equity markets, and are considered a normal feature of functioning markets.","course","Corrections feel worse than the 10 percent number suggests because they arrive without advance notice and are indistinguishable from the beginning of something worse while they are happening. The historical record is clear: corrections are temporary. Every market correction in U.S. history has been followed by recovery to new highs — the uncertainty is always timing, not direction."],
  [9,"Bear Market","/bɛr ˈmɑːrkɪt/","noun","A decline in a broad market index of 20 percent or more from a recent peak, sustained over a period of time. Bear markets are typically associated with economic contraction, rising unemployment, declining corporate earnings, and elevated investor pessimism. The average bear market in U.S. history has lasted approximately ten months.","field","Bear markets are corrections that kept going. They feel different from corrections not because of what has already happened but because of the uncertainty about whether the bottom has been reached. The behavioral challenge of a bear market is that the evidence for buying — deeply discounted prices — arrives at the moment when the emotional resistance to buying is highest. That tension is the defining experience of every bear market."],
  [10,"Bull Market","/bʊl ˈmɑːrkɪt/","noun","A sustained rise in the price of a broad market index, typically defined as a gain of 20 percent or more from a recent low, characterized by investor optimism, rising economic activity, and increasing corporate earnings. Bull markets have historically lasted longer and produced larger gains than bear markets have produced losses.","course","Bull markets are the default state of equity markets. Prices rise more often than they fall, and the gains in up markets have historically exceeded the losses in down markets. The asymmetry is why patient investors are rewarded over time — but patience requires surviving the bear markets that interrupt every bull market without abandoning the position."],
  [11,"Drawdown","/ˈdrɔːdaʊn/","noun","The decline in value of an investment or portfolio from its peak to its subsequent trough, expressed as a percentage. A drawdown begins when a new high is reached and ends when the portfolio recovers to that high. It measures loss from the perspective of an investor who bought at the top.","field","Drawdown is the most investor-relevant measure of loss because it captures what the experience actually felt like. A portfolio that lost 30 percent from its high is a 30 percent drawdown, regardless of where it started. The time spent in drawdown — waiting for recovery — is as important as the magnitude, because duration determines how long an investor must sustain the psychological weight of being down."],
  [12,"Maximum Drawdown","/ˈmæksɪməm ˈdrɔːdaʊn/","noun","The largest peak-to-trough decline in the value of an investment or portfolio over a specified period. Maximum drawdown represents the worst-case loss an investor would have experienced had they purchased at the highest point and held through the lowest point before recovery.","course","Maximum drawdown is the stress test of any investment strategy. It answers the question: what is the worst this has done, and could an investor have survived it without abandoning the strategy? A strategy with attractive average returns but a 60 percent maximum drawdown is only viable for investors who can genuinely hold through that kind of loss — and most cannot. Knowing the maximum drawdown is knowing the emotional price of admission."],
  [13,"Underwater Period","/ˌʌndərˈwɔːtər ˈpɪəriəd/","noun","The span of time during which a portfolio remains below its previous peak value. An investor who bought at a prior high is underwater — holding losses — until the portfolio recovers to that level. The length of the underwater period is a critical dimension of investment risk that average return figures do not capture.","field","Underwater periods test investor resolve more than any market statistic. A portfolio can recover 100 percent of its loss mathematically but still feel like a permanent impairment to the investor who experienced the decline. The Japanese equity market, for example, spent decades underwater following its late 1980s peak. Duration of the underwater period is a risk factor as real as magnitude."],
  [14,"Recovery Time","/rɪˈkʌvəri taɪm/","noun","The time required for an investment or portfolio to return to its prior peak value following a drawdown. Recovery time depends on both the magnitude of the decline — larger declines require proportionally larger subsequent gains — and the return environment during the recovery period.","course","The mathematics of recovery are asymmetric: a 50 percent loss requires a 100 percent gain to break even. A 20 percent loss requires a 25 percent gain. This asymmetry is why preserving capital during downturns is worth more than its face value suggests — avoiding a large loss compounds forward as powerfully as capturing a large gain. Recovery time is the tax that large drawdowns levy on future wealth."],
  [15,"Sequence of Returns Risk","/ˈsiːkwəns əv rɪˈtɜːrnz rɪsk/","noun","The risk that the timing of investment returns — not just their average — significantly affects long-term outcomes, particularly for investors who are withdrawing from their portfolios. A series of poor returns early in retirement depletes the portfolio before recovery can occur, permanently impairing the income it can sustain.","field","Sequence of returns risk is the central risk of retirement. Two retirees with identical average returns over 30 years can experience dramatically different outcomes if one retires into a bear market and the other into a bull market. The early years of retirement are when withdrawals do the most damage — each dollar withdrawn during a down market is a dollar that cannot recover. Managing early retirement market exposure is not timidity; it is understanding the mathematics of when risk is most costly."],
  [16,"Volatility Drag","/ˌvɒləˈtɪlɪti dræɡ/","noun","The reduction in compound return caused by volatility, even when average arithmetic returns remain the same. Because losses require proportionally larger gains to recover, high volatility reduces the geometric mean return below the arithmetic mean return. The larger the volatility, the larger the drag on actual compounded wealth.","course","Volatility drag is why two portfolios with the same average annual return can produce very different terminal wealth. A portfolio returning 10 percent, 10 percent, and 10 percent will end higher than one returning 30 percent, negative 10 percent, and 10 percent — despite identical arithmetic averages. Smoothing returns through diversification and risk management has value that arithmetic averages do not reflect."],
  [17,"Correlation","/ˌkɒrəˈleɪʃən/","noun","A statistical measure of the degree to which two investments move in relation to each other, ranging from negative 1 (perfect inverse movement) to positive 1 (perfect parallel movement). A correlation of zero indicates no consistent relationship. Diversification benefits increase as correlations between holdings decrease.","field","Correlation is the engine of diversification. Holdings that move together provide no cushion when one falls — if everything declines simultaneously, the portfolio falls with it. Holdings that move independently — or inversely — smooth the ride. The challenge is that correlations are not stable: assets that appear uncorrelated in normal markets frequently converge during crises, precisely when diversification is needed most."],
  [18,"Correlation Breakdown","/ˌkɒrəˈleɪʃən ˈbreɪkdaʊn/","noun","The tendency for correlations between asset classes and securities to increase during market crises, often approaching positive 1 even among assets that appeared uncorrelated in calmer conditions. When markets fall sharply, investors tend to sell whatever they can, causing previously uncorrelated assets to decline together.","course","Correlation breakdown is the great disappointment of diversification during crises. The portfolio that looked well-diversified in calm markets suddenly moves as a single unit during the storm. The assets that hold their value during correlation breakdowns — high-quality government bonds, cash, and certain alternatives — earn their place in the portfolio precisely in these moments, even if they drag on returns otherwise."],
  [19,"Systematic Risk","/ˌsɪstəˈmætɪk rɪsk/","noun","Risk that affects the entire market and cannot be eliminated through diversification — also called market risk or non-diversifiable risk. It includes macroeconomic events, interest rate changes, geopolitical disruptions, and pandemics. All portfolios that hold market assets carry some systematic risk; it is the price of participation.","field","Systematic risk is the irreducible risk of investing in markets. No amount of diversification within markets eliminates it — when the market falls broadly, diversified portfolios fall with it. Managing systematic risk requires either reducing market exposure overall, hedging, or accepting it as the cost of long-term participation. Beta measures how much systematic risk a portfolio carries."],
  [20,"Idiosyncratic Risk","/ˌɪdioʊˌsɪŋkrəˈtɪk rɪsk/","noun","Risk specific to an individual company, sector, or security that can be reduced or eliminated through diversification. Poor management decisions, product failures, regulatory actions, and company-specific events are idiosyncratic. A well-diversified portfolio retains systematic risk but substantially eliminates idiosyncratic risk.","course","Idiosyncratic risk is the risk investors take on without compensation when they concentrate in individual securities. Owning 30 or more uncorrelated stocks substantially eliminates company-specific risk — any single company's failure has limited impact on the portfolio. Concentration in a handful of names — or in a single industry — means idiosyncratic risk remains, and the expected return does not increase to compensate for it."],
  [21,"Liquidity Risk","/lɪˈkwɪdɪti rɪsk/","noun","The risk that an investment cannot be sold quickly enough, or at a price close enough to fair value, to meet the investor's needs. Illiquid investments — private equity, real estate, certain bonds — may require significant time to sell or may only be sellable at a steep discount during market stress.","field","Liquidity risk is invisible in normal markets and acute in crises. An investor who held illiquid assets through normal conditions and suddenly needs cash — due to an emergency, a margin call, or a change in circumstances — may be forced to sell at deeply unfavorable prices or simply be unable to sell at all. Matching the liquidity of investments to the liquidity needs of the investor is a foundational risk management principle."],
  [22,"Counterparty Risk","/ˈkaʊntərpɑːrti rɪsk/","noun","The risk that the other party in a financial contract — a derivative counterparty, a clearing house, a bond issuer, or a financial institution — will fail to meet its obligations. Counterparty risk is distinct from market risk because it relates to the reliability of a specific entity, not the movement of prices.","course","Counterparty risk is most visible in derivatives, where the contract's value depends entirely on the counterparty's ability and willingness to pay. It also appears in bonds — which are promises to pay — and in any arrangement where performance depends on another party. The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated how counterparty risk can cascade through financial systems when multiple institutions simultaneously become unable to honor commitments."],
  [23,"Tail Risk","/teɪl rɪsk/","noun","The risk of rare but extreme outcomes that lie in the tails of the return distribution — beyond what standard deviation would predict. Tail events occur more frequently than a normal distribution suggests because financial returns exhibit fat tails: extreme events happen more often than standard models assume.","field","Tail risk is the risk of the event you did not plan for because it was too unlikely. Markets crash further than models predict, correlations spike toward 1, and liquidity disappears simultaneously. The lesson of every major market crisis is that tail risk was systematically underestimated. Managing tail risk does not mean predicting crises — it means building portfolios that can survive them."],
  [24,"Fat Tails","/fæt teɪlz/","noun","A property of return distributions in which extreme outcomes — both gains and losses — occur more frequently than a normal bell curve distribution would predict. Financial markets exhibit fat tails because human behavior, feedback loops, and structural factors produce more outlier events than random statistical models assume.","course","Fat tails are why standard deviation alone underestimates real investment risk. The normal distribution assigns near-zero probability to events that happen every decade or so in financial markets. A 5-standard-deviation market event would be essentially impossible under a normal distribution — yet such events occur with regularity. Accounting for fat tails requires using different models and maintaining more resilience than standard volatility measures suggest is necessary."],
  [25,"Black Swan","/blæk swɒn/","noun","A term popularized by Nassim Taleb referring to an extremely rare, high-impact event that was not predicted by conventional models — and that, in retrospect, appears to have been inevitable. Black swans are characterized by their rarity, their massive impact, and the human tendency to construct explanations for them after the fact.","field","The black swan concept is a corrective to overconfident prediction. Financial models are built on historical data and assume the future will resemble the past. Black swans are the events that don't — the 2008 financial crisis, the COVID pandemic's market impact, the 1987 single-day crash. The honest response is not to predict them but to build portfolios robust enough to survive them."],
  [26,"Regression to the Mean","/rɪˈɡrɛʃən tə ðə miːn/","noun","The statistical tendency for extreme values — both high and low — to be followed by values closer to the long-term average. In investing, it describes the phenomenon where assets or strategies that have performed unusually well or poorly tend to revert toward their historical average over time.","course","Regression to the mean is one of the most consistently misapplied concepts in investing. It does not predict when reversion will occur — only that extreme performance tends not to persist indefinitely. The fund that topped the rankings last year is more likely to be average next year than to repeat. The asset class that has significantly outperformed its long-run average is more likely to underperform going forward than to continue its streak."],
  [27,"Mean Reversion","/miːn rɪˈvɜːrʒən/","noun","The tendency of asset prices or returns to move back toward their historical average over time, following periods of significant deviation above or below that average. Mean reversion underpins value investing and contrarian strategies — the belief that what has fallen significantly will recover and what has risen significantly will correct.","field","Mean reversion is a powerful force in markets over long periods — and a dangerous assumption over short ones. The timing of reversion is unknowable. A cheap asset can become cheaper; an expensive one can remain expensive for years. Strategies built on mean reversion require the patience and capital to wait for reversion to occur, without certainty about when that will be."],
  [28,"Momentum vs Mean Reversion","/moʊˈmɛntəm vɜːrsəs miːn rɪˈvɜːrʒən/","noun","The tension between two observed market phenomena: momentum — the tendency for recent winners to continue outperforming in the near term — and mean reversion — the tendency for extreme performance to eventually normalize. They are both real and appear to operate on different time horizons: momentum over months, mean reversion over years.","course","Momentum and mean reversion are not contradictory — they operate on different clocks. A stock that has risen strongly may continue rising in the coming months (momentum) while still being likely to underperform over the coming decade as it regresses toward average (mean reversion). Understanding which force dominates over which time horizon prevents the error of applying the wrong lens."],
  [29,"Loss Aversion","/lɒs əˈvɜːrʒən/","noun","A behavioral tendency, documented in prospect theory, in which the psychological pain of a loss is approximately twice as intense as the pleasure from an equivalent gain. Loss aversion causes investors to hold losing positions too long, sell winning positions too early, and make decisions based on avoiding regret rather than maximizing expected value.","field","Loss aversion is the most consequential behavioral bias in investing. It explains why investors sell into falling markets — the pain of watching losses accumulate overwhelms the rational case for holding — and why they hesitate to invest in declining markets even when prices represent genuine value. Recognizing loss aversion doesn't eliminate it, but it creates a useful pause: is this a rational assessment, or is the pain of recent losses driving the decision?"],
  [30,"Anchoring","/ˈæŋkərɪŋ/","noun","A cognitive bias in which investors rely too heavily on a specific reference point — the price paid for a security, a recent high, or an arbitrary number — when making decisions about that investment. The anchor distorts judgment by making the reference point more relevant than current fundamentals warrant.","course","Anchoring is why investors say they will sell a stock when it gets back to what they paid for it — a decision criterion based entirely on their own cost, which has no relationship to the stock's actual value or future prospects. The market does not know or care what anyone paid. Decisions should be based on current value and forward expectations, not on the psychological weight of a past price."],
  [31,"Recency Bias","/ˈriːsənsi ˈbaɪəs/","noun","The tendency to give excessive weight to recent events and experiences when forecasting the future, assuming that what has happened lately is more likely to continue than the long-term base rate would suggest. Recency bias makes investors extrapolate bull markets into optimism and bear markets into pessimism.","field","Recency bias is the engine of bubbles and panics. Near the peak of a bull market, investors extrapolate recent gains and increase risk — at the worst possible time. Near the bottom of a bear market, they extrapolate recent losses and reduce risk — also at the worst possible time. The antidote is a long perspective that holds recent experience against the full historical range."],
  [32,"Overconfidence","/ˌoʊvərˈkɒnfɪdəns/","noun","A well-documented cognitive bias in which investors overestimate the accuracy of their own knowledge and the precision of their forecasts. Overconfident investors trade too frequently, take excessive risk, and underestimate uncertainty. Studies consistently show that most investors believe they are above-average market participants — a statistical impossibility.","course","Overconfidence is particularly dangerous in investing because the feedback loop is slow and noisy. Unlike skills where performance is immediately apparent, investment performance takes years to evaluate accurately. This delay allows overconfident beliefs to persist long past the point where evidence should have corrected them. Humility about what is knowable is not a personality trait — it is a risk management strategy."],
  [33,"Herding","/ˈhɜːrdɪŋ/","noun","The tendency of investors to follow the crowd — buying what others are buying and selling what others are selling — often without independent analysis. Herding amplifies market movements: it drives prices above fundamental value in bull markets and below fundamental value in bear markets.","field","Herding is rational at the individual level and destructive at the aggregate level. Following the crowd reduces the risk of being spectacularly wrong relative to peers — no one gets fired for doing what everyone else is doing. But collective movement away from fundamentals creates the bubbles and panics that produce the most severe market dislocations. Disciplined independent analysis is the structural defense against herding."],
  [34,"Capitulation","/kəˌpɪtʃʊˈleɪʃən/","noun","The point at which investors who have been holding through a market decline give up and sell, typically producing a surge of selling volume and sharp price decline. Capitulation is often associated with market bottoms — once those who were going to sell have sold, selling pressure exhausts itself and the path of least resistance shifts upward.","course","Capitulation is recognizable in retrospect and nearly invisible in real time. The surge of selling that marks a bottom looks identical to the continued selling that marks a decline in its early stages. What distinguishes capitulation is the exhaustion of sellers — when even the most patient investors have surrendered. The emotional state at a capitulation bottom is not bearishness; it is resignation. That resignation, historically, has been the signal to lean in.","To surrender a held position during a market decline — selling after absorbing significant losses, driven by the belief that further losses are likely or the emotional weight of continuing to hold has become unbearable. An investor who capitulates converts a paper loss into a realized one and exits at or near a price trough.","field","To capitulate is to stop fighting the loss. The decision to sell arrives not from analysis but from exhaustion — the accumulated pain of watching a position decline, the fear that it will keep falling, the need to make the discomfort stop. Capitulation typically occurs near bottoms precisely because that is when the emotional pressure is greatest. The investor who sells is often selling to someone who is buying — and the buyer is usually right."],
  [35,"Panic Selling","/ˈpænɪk ˈsɛlɪŋ/","noun","The rapid, fear-driven liquidation of investments without regard to fundamental value, typically triggered by sharp market declines, alarming news, or the sight of others selling. Panic selling accelerates declines and locks in losses that would otherwise be temporary.","field","Panic selling is where the behavioral risk of investing becomes the financial risk. A portfolio that falls 30 percent recovers — eventually. A portfolio that falls 30 percent and then is liquidated does not. The investor who sells during a panic locks in the loss permanently and then faces the additional challenge of deciding when to re-enter a market that may have already begun recovering. The most expensive four words in investing are not buy high, sell low — they are I can't take it anymore."],
  [36,"Safe Haven Assets","/seɪf ˈheɪvən ˈæsɛts/","noun","Investments that investors seek during periods of market stress because they are expected to retain or increase in value when riskier assets decline. Common safe havens include U.S. Treasury bonds, cash, gold, the Swiss franc, and the Japanese yen. They are not permanently safe — they can decline in non-crisis conditions — but they offer relative stability during turbulence.","course","Safe haven assets earn their place in portfolios by performing when other assets don't — which means they may underperform when other assets do. U.S. Treasuries declined in 2022 alongside equities because the shock was inflation-driven, not growth-driven — a reminder that safe haven status is conditional on the nature of the crisis. Maintaining some allocation to safe havens accepts a performance drag in exchange for resilience in the specific scenarios where resilience matters most."],
  [37,"Flight to Quality","/flaɪt tə ˈkwɒlɪti/","noun","The movement of investor capital from riskier assets — equities, high-yield bonds, emerging markets — to safer assets — government bonds, cash, gold — during periods of market stress or economic uncertainty. Flight to quality drives up the prices of safe assets and drives down the prices of riskier ones simultaneously.","field","Flight to quality is the mechanism through which fear propagates through markets. As investors sell risk assets and buy safe assets, yields on government bonds fall, spreads on riskier bonds widen, and equity prices decline — simultaneously. Understanding flight to quality explains the correlations that emerge during crises and clarifies why portfolio construction during calm markets cannot simply assume that stress conditions will look like calm ones."],
  [38,"Stress Testing","/strɛs ˈtɛstɪŋ/","noun","The process of evaluating a portfolio's or financial institution's performance under hypothetical adverse scenarios — a severe recession, a sharp rate increase, a market crash, or a specific historical episode. Stress tests reveal vulnerabilities that normal-condition analysis may miss.","course","Stress testing is the discipline of asking what if before events force the question. Running a portfolio through the 2008 scenario, the 2020 crash, or the 2022 rate shock reveals concentrations and correlations that only appear under pressure. It does not predict the future — the next crisis will likely look different from the last one — but it prevents the error of having a portfolio optimized only for the conditions that currently exist.","To subject a portfolio or financial plan to hypothetical adverse scenarios in order to reveal how it would perform under stress conditions. An advisor who stress-tests a portfolio is deliberately looking for the vulnerabilities that calm-market analysis would miss.","course","To stress-test a portfolio is to run it through the scenarios you hope never happen — and see what breaks. The goal is not to predict the next crisis but to identify exposures that only become visible under pressure: concentrations that look fine until they don't, correlations that hold until they don't, liquidity assumptions that are comfortable until they aren't. The discomfort of the exercise is the point."],
  [39,"Scenario Analysis","/sɪˈnɛərioʊ əˈnælɪsɪs/","noun","The evaluation of a portfolio or financial plan under a range of defined possible future conditions — optimistic, base, and pessimistic cases. Unlike stress testing, which focuses on adverse outcomes, scenario analysis spans the full range of plausible futures and examines the portfolio's behavior across each.","field","Scenario analysis broadens the frame from what could go wrong to what could happen. It prevents both excessive pessimism — building a portfolio for a permanent crisis — and excessive optimism — assuming the recent past will continue indefinitely. Seeing how a portfolio behaves across multiple scenarios provides a more complete picture of its resilience and its exposure to specific outcomes."],
  [40,"Monte Carlo Simulation","/ˈmɒnti ˈkɑːrloʊ ˌsɪmjʊˈleɪʃən/","noun","A computational technique that models the probability of different outcomes by running thousands of simulations using random variables within specified parameters. In financial planning, Monte Carlo simulations estimate the probability that a retirement plan will succeed — that the portfolio will survive a specified spending rate across a range of return sequences and market conditions.","course","Monte Carlo simulation converts the unknowable future into a probability distribution of outcomes. A plan with a 90 percent success rate does not guarantee success — it means that in 90 percent of the simulated scenarios, the portfolio survived. The 10 percent failure scenarios are real possibilities. Monte Carlo is more honest than simple average-return projections because it accounts for the variability of returns, not just their average."],
  [41,"Rebalancing","/ˌriːˈbælənsɪŋ/","noun","The process of realigning a portfolio's actual asset allocation back to its target by selling assets that have grown above their target weight and buying assets that have fallen below it. Rebalancing maintains consistent risk exposure as market movements drift the portfolio away from its intended composition.","field","Rebalancing forces a mechanical form of disciplined investing: sell what has risen and buy what has fallen. It is the implementation of buy low, sell high without requiring a forecast. Done consistently, it maintains the portfolio's risk profile and captures a rebalancing premium over time — though it also creates taxable events in taxable accounts, which must be weighed against its benefits.","To realign a portfolio's actual asset allocation back to its target by selling positions that have grown above their intended weight and buying positions that have fallen below it. An investor who rebalances is deliberately selling recent winners and adding to recent laggards — enforcing discipline in the direction opposite to market momentum.","course","To rebalance is to act against the grain. Markets move positions away from their targets by rewarding some and penalizing others. Rebalancing redirects capital from what has done well — trimming it back — toward what has done poorly — adding to it. The mechanics are straightforward; the psychology is not. Buying what has recently fallen feels wrong. That discomfort is precisely why the discipline has value."],
  [42,"Rebalancing Bands","/ˌriːˈbælənsɪŋ bændz/","noun","Threshold rules that trigger rebalancing when any asset class drifts beyond a defined percentage above or below its target weight — for example, 5 percentage points — rather than on a fixed calendar schedule. Bands-based rebalancing responds to market movements rather than the passage of time.","course","Bands-based rebalancing is more responsive than calendar rebalancing and typically more tax-efficient, because it triggers fewer transactions in calm markets where little drift occurs. The tradeoff is the need to monitor allocations continuously rather than simply rebalancing on a fixed schedule. For most investors and advisors, a combination — calendar review with band triggers — provides both structure and responsiveness."],
  [43,"Tactical De-Risking","/ˈtæktɪkəl diː-ˈrɪskɪŋ/","noun","The deliberate reduction of portfolio risk during periods of elevated market stress or uncertainty — by reducing equity exposure, increasing cash or bonds, or adding hedges — with the intention of re-risking when conditions stabilize. It is a discretionary, short-term deviation from the strategic allocation.","field","Tactical de-risking is appealing in theory and difficult in practice. It requires two correct decisions: when to reduce risk and when to restore it. History shows that most tactical risk reduction happens after markets have already fallen — locking in losses — and most re-risking happens after markets have already recovered — capturing less of the rebound. For most investors, maintaining a strategic allocation that can survive a crisis is more reliable than attempting to navigate around it.","To deliberately reduce portfolio risk — by trimming equity exposure, adding cash or bonds, or implementing hedges — during a period of elevated uncertainty, with the intention of restoring risk when conditions stabilize. An investor who de-risks tactically is making an active choice to deviate from their long-term allocation in response to near-term conditions.","course","To de-risk is to make two bets simultaneously: that now is the right time to reduce exposure, and that you will know when to restore it. Both bets must be correct for the trade to add value. History is not encouraging — most tactical de-risking occurs after markets have already fallen, and most re-risking after markets have already recovered. The discipline of not de-risking, difficult as it is, has outperformed most attempts to time the trade."],
  [44,"Liquidity Ladder","/lɪˈkwɪdɪti ˈlædər/","noun","A portfolio structure that divides assets into tiers based on time horizon and liquidity needs — cash for near-term expenses, short-term bonds for intermediate needs, and long-term growth assets for the distant future. The ladder ensures that short-term needs can be met without selling long-term assets during market downturns.","course","A liquidity ladder is time-horizon management made visible. By ensuring that near-term spending needs are funded from liquid assets, the investor can hold through market volatility in the long-term portion without being forced to sell at depressed prices. The psychological effect is as important as the financial one: knowing that the next two or three years of expenses are covered in stable assets makes it easier to leave the growth portfolio alone during a storm."],
  [45,"Volatility Harvesting","/ˌvɒləˈtɪlɪti ˈhɑːrvɪstɪŋ/","noun","An investment strategy that seeks to benefit from volatility through systematic rebalancing, options strategies, or volatility-linked instruments. By rebalancing consistently into declining assets and out of rising ones, a portfolio can earn a volatility premium over time — generating returns above a simple buy-and-hold approach in volatile, range-bound markets.","field","Volatility harvesting converts the enemy into an ally. Rather than treating volatility as a threat to be endured, it reframes volatility as the mechanism by which systematic rebalancing adds value. The strategy works best when markets oscillate rather than trend continuously in one direction — and it reinforces the discipline of rebalancing at the moments when it feels most counterintuitive, which are often the moments when it adds the most value."]
];

const investingTerms = [
  [1,"Asset Classes","/ˈæsɛt ˈklæsɪz/","noun","The broad categories into which investments are grouped based on shared characteristics, risk profiles, and market behavior. The primary asset classes are equities, fixed income, cash and cash equivalents, real assets, and alternatives. Diversifying across asset classes reduces the risk that any single category's decline will devastate the whole portfolio.","field","Asset class is the first level of portfolio organization — before sectors, geographies, or individual securities. Understanding which class an investment belongs to clarifies what role it plays, what risk it carries, and how it is likely to behave relative to the rest of the portfolio."],
  [2,"Equities","/ˈɛkwɪtiz/","noun","Ownership interests in companies, representing a claim on assets and earnings. Equities include common stock, preferred stock, and equity-based funds. As a class, equities offer the highest long-term return potential among major asset classes and carry the highest short-term volatility.","course","Equities are ownership — not lending. When you hold equities, you participate in the company's success and its failures. The long-term historical return premium of equities over bonds and cash reflects the compensation investors demand for accepting that participation in uncertainty."],
  [3,"Fixed Income","/fɪkst ˈɪnkʌm/","noun","Debt instruments that pay a defined stream of interest over a specified period, returning principal at maturity. Bonds, notes, bills, and certificates of deposit are fixed income instruments. They provide more predictable income than equities and generally exhibit lower volatility, at the cost of lower long-term return.","field","Fixed income is lending — not owning. The investor provides capital in exchange for a contractual promise of repayment with interest. That contract is the source of both the predictability and the ceiling on return: the lender cannot participate in the borrower's upside beyond the agreed interest."],
  [4,"Alternatives","/ɔːlˈtɜːrnətɪvz/","noun","Investment categories outside traditional equities and fixed income, including hedge funds, private equity, private credit, commodities, real assets, and structured products. Alternatives are often characterized by lower liquidity, less regulatory transparency, and return profiles that may behave differently than public markets.","course","Alternatives entered mainstream portfolio construction as investors sought diversification beyond stocks and bonds. Their value depends on genuinely low correlation to traditional assets — if they move with the market during stress, the diversification benefit disappears precisely when it was needed most. Liquidity constraints and complexity are the tradeoffs for potential diversification."],
  [5,"Real Assets","/riːl ˈæsɛts/","noun","Tangible or physical assets that have intrinsic value due to their substance and properties — real estate, infrastructure, timberland, farmland, and commodities. Real assets often provide inflation protection because their value tends to rise with prices.","field","Real assets connect the portfolio to the physical world. Their inflation-hedging properties make them particularly relevant in periods of sustained price increases, when traditional fixed income loses purchasing power. The tradeoff is illiquidity — real assets are not as easily sold as publicly traded securities."],
  [6,"Market Capitalization","/ˈmɑːrkɪt ˌkæpɪtəlaɪˈzeɪʃən/","noun","The total market value of a company's outstanding shares, calculated by multiplying the current share price by the total number of shares outstanding. Market cap is used to classify companies by size — large cap, mid cap, and small cap — and to weight index constituents.","course","Market cap is the market's current verdict on a company's total value. It is a snapshot, not a permanent classification — companies move between size categories as their prices and share counts change. Cap-weighted indexes give larger companies more influence on index returns, which has significant implications for how indexes behave."],
  [7,"Large Cap","/lɑːrdʒ kæp/","noun","Companies with market capitalizations generally above $10 billion. Large cap stocks typically represent established businesses with stable earnings, broad analyst coverage, and high liquidity. Major indexes like the S&P 500 are composed primarily of large cap companies.","field","Large cap companies dominate most equity portfolios by weight because they dominate cap-weighted indexes. Their size brings stability and liquidity — but also limits the potential for dramatic growth. The largest companies in the world are already large; becoming ten times larger is harder than it was at a smaller scale."],
  [8,"Mid Cap","/mɪd kæp/","noun","Companies with market capitalizations generally between $2 billion and $10 billion. Mid cap stocks occupy a historically favorable position — large enough to have demonstrated viability, small enough to have meaningful growth runway. They have historically outperformed large caps over long periods with modestly higher volatility.","course","Mid cap is sometimes called the sweet spot of the market — past the startup risk of small caps, not yet constrained by the size limitations of large caps. The tradeoff is less analyst coverage and slightly lower liquidity than large cap, which creates both additional risk and additional opportunity for informed investors."],
  [9,"Small Cap","/smɔːl kæp/","noun","Companies with market capitalizations generally between $300 million and $2 billion. Small cap stocks carry higher volatility and less liquidity than larger companies, but have historically offered higher long-term returns as compensation for that additional risk.","field","Small caps are where growth potential and risk concentration collide most visibly. Individual small cap companies fail at higher rates than large companies — but as a diversified group, they have historically rewarded patient investors who could tolerate the volatility. The key word is diversified: concentrated small cap exposure is a different proposition entirely."],
  [10,"Growth vs Value","/ɡroʊθ vɜːrsəs ˈvæljuː/","noun","Two broad investment styles defined by the characteristics of the stocks selected. Growth investing targets companies expected to increase earnings significantly faster than average, typically trading at premium valuations. Value investing targets companies trading below their estimated intrinsic value, often with lower price-to-earnings ratios and more modest growth expectations.","course","Growth and value are not strategies — they are orientations. Each has periods of significant outperformance and underperformance relative to the other. Blending both within a portfolio, or simply holding a broad market index, avoids the timing risk of making a definitive bet on either style."],
  [11,"Earnings Per Share","/ˈɜːrnɪŋz pɜːr ʃɛr/","noun","A company's net profit divided by the number of outstanding shares. EPS measures profitability on a per-share basis, making it comparable across companies of different sizes. It is one of the most widely referenced metrics in equity analysis.","field","EPS is the denominator of the price-to-earnings ratio and a primary input in most equity valuation frameworks. Rising EPS over time, combined with a stable or expanding valuation multiple, is the engine of long-term stock price appreciation. Falling EPS or a contracting multiple works in the opposite direction."],
  [12,"Price-to-Earnings Ratio","/praɪs-tə-ˈɜːrnɪŋz ˈreɪʃioʊ/","noun","A valuation metric calculated by dividing the current share price by earnings per share. The P/E ratio reflects how much investors are willing to pay for each dollar of a company's earnings. A high P/E suggests high growth expectations or premium valuation; a low P/E may indicate undervaluation or low growth prospects.","course","The P/E ratio is the most commonly cited valuation metric in equity markets — and one of the most commonly misused. A high P/E is not inherently expensive if earnings are growing rapidly; a low P/E is not inherently cheap if earnings are deteriorating. Context — growth rate, industry, interest rate environment — is essential to interpretation."],
  [13,"Price-to-Book Ratio","/praɪs-tə-bʊk ˈreɪʃioʊ/","noun","A valuation metric that compares a company's market price per share to its book value per share — the net assets of the company divided by shares outstanding. A P/B below 1 suggests the market values the company at less than its accounting net worth; a high P/B reflects premium valuation relative to assets.","field","Price-to-book is most meaningful for asset-intensive businesses — banks, manufacturers, real estate companies — where the balance sheet closely reflects actual value. For knowledge or service companies whose value resides in intangibles, brand, and human capital, book value is a poor proxy for true worth and P/B loses much of its interpretive power."],
  [14,"Dividend Yield","/ˈdɪvɪdɛnd jiːld/","noun","The annual dividend paid per share divided by the current share price, expressed as a percentage. It measures the income return component of an equity investment, independent of any price appreciation.","course","Dividend yield tells you what the stock pays in income relative to what you pay for it. A high yield can signal attractive income — or it can signal that the share price has fallen because the dividend is at risk. A falling price mechanically increases the yield, which is why unusually high yields often warrant scrutiny rather than celebration."],
  [15,"Total Return vs Price Return","/ˈtoʊtəl rɪˈtɜːrn vɜːrsəs praɪs rɪˈtɜːrn/","noun","Total return measures the complete gain from an investment, including both price appreciation and income received — dividends, interest, or distributions. Price return measures only the change in price, excluding income. Over long periods, the reinvestment of dividends accounts for a substantial portion of total equity returns.","field","The gap between price return and total return is invisible in day-to-day market reporting but compounding over decades. A stock index that appears to have returned modestly based on price appreciation may have delivered dramatically more in total return once dividends are included and reinvested. Comparing investments on price return alone understates the actual experience."],
  [16,"Stock Split","/stɒk splɪt/","noun","A corporate action that increases the number of shares outstanding by issuing additional shares to existing shareholders in a fixed ratio — for example, two shares for every one held. The share price adjusts proportionally, leaving total market capitalization unchanged.","course","A stock split changes nothing fundamental about a company — total value, earnings, and ownership percentage are unaffected. It lowers the per-share price, which can improve liquidity and accessibility for smaller investors. Splits often follow strong price appreciation and are occasionally interpreted as a positive signal, though the signal is the prior performance, not the split itself."],
  [17,"Reverse Split","/rɪˈvɜːrs splɪt/","noun","A corporate action that reduces the number of shares outstanding by consolidating existing shares into fewer shares at a higher price — for example, one share for every ten held. Like a forward split, total market capitalization is unchanged.","field","Reverse splits are most often executed to bring a stock's price above a minimum threshold — commonly required by exchanges to maintain listing. They frequently accompany financial distress or sustained underperformance. A reverse split does not improve the company's fundamentals; it changes only the presentation of those fundamentals."],
  [18,"Dividend Reinvestment","/ˈdɪvɪdɛnd ˌriːɪnˈvɛstmənt/","noun","The automatic use of dividend payments to purchase additional shares of the same stock or fund, rather than receiving the cash. Most brokerages and many companies offer dividend reinvestment plans (DRIPs) that execute this automatically, often without transaction fees.","course","Dividend reinvestment is one of the quietest and most effective compounding mechanisms available. Each reinvested dividend buys more shares, which generate more dividends, which buy more shares. Over decades, this cycle contributes substantially to total return — particularly in dividend-paying indexes where yields compound continuously without requiring active management.","To automatically use dividend payments to purchase additional shares of the same investment rather than receiving the cash. An investor who reinvests dividends is engaging the full compounding mechanism — each additional share generates additional dividends that buy more shares in turn.","field","To reinvest dividends is to let compounding do its full work. The cash doesn't leave the position — it re-enters it, purchasing fractional shares that participate in future appreciation and future dividends. The effect is invisible in any single quarter and transformative over decades. It is one of the most powerful passive decisions available."],
  [19,"Momentum","/moʊˈmɛntəm/","noun","An investment factor based on the observation that securities that have performed well recently tend to continue outperforming in the near term, and securities that have performed poorly tend to continue underperforming. Momentum strategies systematically buy recent winners and sell or avoid recent losers.","field","Momentum is one of the most robustly documented factors in academic finance — and one of the most psychologically uncomfortable to follow, because it requires buying what has already risen and selling what has already fallen. It works until it reverses sharply, which it does periodically and without warning. Position sizing and diversification are essential in momentum strategies."],
  [20,"Factor Investing","/ˈfæktər ɪnˈvɛstɪŋ/","noun","An investment approach that systematically targets specific characteristics — factors — that research has identified as drivers of risk-adjusted returns. Common factors include value, momentum, quality, low volatility, and size. Factor investing sits between passive indexing and active stock selection.","course","Factor investing operationalizes academic research into portfolio construction. Rather than selecting individual stocks, factor investors tilt toward the characteristics that have historically been associated with excess returns. The persistent debate is whether factors persist because they represent genuine risk premia or because they have been arbitraged away since their discovery."],
  [21,"ESG","/iː-ɛs-dʒiː/","noun","Environmental, Social, and Governance — a framework for evaluating companies based on their practices and performance across these three dimensions. ESG investing incorporates these criteria alongside financial metrics in investment selection, either to align with values, manage non-financial risk, or both.","field","ESG means different things to different investors. For some it is primarily values alignment — excluding industries or companies inconsistent with their principles. For others it is risk management — identifying companies whose governance or environmental exposure creates financial vulnerability. The two motivations are related but distinct, and the investment products built around ESG vary significantly in their methodology."],
  [22,"Investment Grade","/ɪnˈvɛstmənt ɡreɪd/","noun","A credit quality designation for bonds rated BBB- or higher by Standard & Poor's or Baa3 or higher by Moody's, indicating a relatively low risk of default. Investment grade bonds are considered suitable for conservative investors and are held by many institutional investors with credit quality requirements.","course","Investment grade is the dividing line in bond markets between conservative and speculative credit. Below this threshold lies high-yield or junk territory. The designation affects who can hold the bond, how it is priced, and how it behaves in market stress. Investment grade bonds lose value when rates rise but rarely fail to pay."],
  [23,"Credit Rating","/ˈkrɛdɪt ˈreɪtɪŋ/","noun","An assessment of the creditworthiness of a bond issuer — its ability and willingness to meet its debt obligations. Ratings are issued by agencies including Standard & Poor's, Moody's, and Fitch, on scales ranging from AAA (highest quality) to D (default). Ratings influence the interest rate the issuer must pay.","field","Credit ratings are opinions, not guarantees. The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated that even AAA-rated instruments can fail catastrophically when the underlying assumptions are wrong. Ratings are a starting point for credit analysis, not an endpoint — they reflect publicly available information and agency methodology, which may lag market reality."],
  [24,"High-Yield Bond","/haɪ-jiːld bɒnd/","noun","A bond rated below investment grade — BB+ or lower by S&P, Ba1 or lower by Moody's. Also called junk bonds. High-yield bonds offer higher interest rates to compensate investors for the greater risk of default. They behave more like equities than investment grade bonds in periods of market stress.","course","High-yield bonds occupy the riskier end of the fixed income spectrum. Their higher coupons attract income-oriented investors, but the default risk and price volatility are meaningful. In recessions and credit crises, high-yield spreads widen dramatically — prices fall — as investors demand more compensation for risk they had previously underpriced."],
  [25,"Convertible Bond","/kənˈvɜːrtɪbəl bɒnd/","noun","A hybrid security — a bond that can be converted into a specified number of shares of the issuing company's common stock at the holder's option, under defined conditions. Convertibles offer bond-like downside protection with equity-like upside participation if the stock performs well.","field","Convertible bonds sit at the intersection of debt and equity. They provide a floor from the bond component and a ceiling that rises with the stock. The tradeoff is a lower coupon than a straight bond and a higher price than the conversion value of the equity alone. They are complex instruments whose value depends on both interest rates and stock price movement."],
  [26,"Preferred Stock","/prɪˈfɜːrd stɒk/","noun","A class of equity that has priority over common stock in dividend payments and in claims on assets if the company is liquidated. Preferred dividends are typically fixed, like a bond coupon, and must be paid before common dividends. Preferred stock generally does not carry voting rights.","course","Preferred stock is a hybrid — more like a bond in its income behavior, more like equity in its legal structure. It sits between bonds and common stock in the capital structure's priority hierarchy. For income-oriented investors, preferred stock can offer higher yields than investment grade bonds — at the cost of lower priority than bondholders and limited upside compared to common equity."],
  [27,"Duration","/djʊˈreɪʃən/","noun","A measure of a bond's sensitivity to interest rate changes, expressed in years. It represents the weighted average time to receive all cash flows from the bond. A bond with a duration of 5 years will lose approximately 5 percent of its value for each 1 percent rise in interest rates, and gain approximately 5 percent for each 1 percent decline.","field","Duration is the single most important risk metric for fixed income portfolios. It tells you how much rate movement hurts or helps. Long-duration bonds are more sensitive — they move more for a given rate change. Short-duration bonds are more stable. Managing portfolio duration is how fixed income investors manage interest rate risk."],
  [28,"Convexity","/kənˈvɛksɪti/","noun","A measure of the curvature in the relationship between a bond's price and interest rates. While duration provides a linear approximation of price sensitivity, convexity captures the fact that the relationship is curved — bonds gain more in price when rates fall than they lose when rates rise by the same amount. Positive convexity is generally desirable.","course","Convexity is the refinement that duration alone misses. Two bonds with identical duration can behave differently when rates move significantly, because their convexity differs. Positive convexity means the bond outperforms the duration estimate in both directions — it is a favorable characteristic that typically comes at a price in the form of lower yield."],
  [29,"Yield to Maturity","/jiːld tə məˈtjʊərɪti/","noun","The total annualized return an investor would receive by holding a bond from the current date to its maturity date, assuming all coupon payments are received and reinvested at the same rate. YTM is the most complete single measure of a bond's return potential.","field","Yield to maturity is the bond equivalent of expected total return. It accounts for the current price, the coupon, the time to maturity, and the return of principal — and rolls them into a single annualized figure. When comparing bonds of similar credit quality and maturity, YTM is the standard measure of relative value."],
  [30,"Current Yield","/ˈkʌrənt jiːld/","noun","A bond's annual coupon payment divided by its current market price, expressed as a percentage. Current yield measures only the income component of a bond's return — it ignores any gain or loss from price movement or the effect of holding to maturity.","course","Current yield is a partial measure — useful for comparing income across bonds at a snapshot in time, but incomplete as a measure of total return. A bond purchased at a discount to face value has a higher current yield than yield to maturity would suggest, because it ignores the additional return from receiving face value at maturity."],
  [31,"Spread","/sprɛd/","noun","The difference in yield between two bonds — most commonly between a corporate or other non-government bond and a comparable U.S. Treasury bond. The spread represents the additional yield investors demand as compensation for taking on credit risk or other risk relative to the risk-free benchmark.","field","Spreads are the market's real-time pricing of credit risk. When spreads widen, investors are demanding more compensation for risk — often a signal of increasing concern about credit conditions or economic stress. When spreads narrow, confidence is rising. Watching spread movements provides a continuous read on market risk appetite that equity prices alone don't capture."],
  [32,"Basis Points","/ˈbeɪsɪs pɔɪnts/","noun","A unit of measurement equal to one one-hundredth of a percentage point, or 0.01 percent. Used universally in finance to express changes in interest rates, yields, spreads, and fees. One hundred basis points equals one percentage point.","course","Basis points eliminate ambiguity in discussions of small percentage changes. A rate moving from 4.00 percent to 4.25 percent rose 25 basis points — cleaner than saying it rose 0.25 percent. In contexts where fractions of a percent matter enormously — bond markets, fee comparisons, central bank policy — basis points are the standard unit of precision."],
  [33,"Yield Curve","/jiːld kɜːrv/","noun","A graph plotting the yields of bonds of the same credit quality across different maturities — typically U.S. Treasuries from three months to thirty years. A normal yield curve slopes upward, with longer maturities offering higher yields. The shape of the curve reflects market expectations for future interest rates and economic conditions.","field","The yield curve is one of the most information-dense charts in finance. Its shape summarizes market expectations about growth, inflation, and monetary policy across time horizons. A steep curve suggests expectations of stronger growth and higher future rates. A flat or inverted curve often precedes economic slowdown. Learning to read the curve adds a macro dimension to any investment perspective."],
  [34,"Inverted Yield Curve","/ɪnˈvɜːrtɪd jiːld kɜːrv/","noun","A condition in which short-term interest rates are higher than long-term rates, producing a downward-sloping yield curve. The inversion of the two-year and ten-year Treasury yields has historically been one of the most reliable predictors of recession, typically occurring twelve to eighteen months before an economic contraction.","course","An inverted yield curve captures a specific market belief: that current rates are high enough to slow the economy, and that future rates will need to fall. It is not a guarantee of recession — it is a signal that has preceded recessions with notable regularity. For long-term investors, it warrants attention without necessarily requiring action."],
  [35,"Index Fund","/ˈɪndɛks fʌnd/","noun","A fund designed to track the performance of a specific market index — such as the S&P 500 or the total bond market — by holding the same securities in the same proportions as the index. Index funds are passively managed, requiring minimal trading and typically carrying very low expense ratios.","field","Index funds democratized broad market participation. Before them, matching market returns required active management with high fees and uncertain results. An index fund delivers what the market delivers — no more, no less — at minimal cost. The profound implication: most active managers, after fees, underperform their benchmark index over time."],
  [36,"ETF","/iː-tiː-ɛf/","noun","Exchange-Traded Fund — a fund that holds a collection of securities and trades on an exchange like a stock throughout the trading day. Most ETFs track an index passively, but actively managed ETFs also exist. ETFs combine the diversification of a mutual fund with the intraday liquidity of a stock.","course","ETFs changed portfolio construction by making virtually every asset class, sector, geography, and strategy accessible in a single tradeable instrument. Their tax efficiency — through the in-kind creation and redemption mechanism — and typically low expense ratios made them the dominant vehicle for index investing. The ability to trade intraday is a feature that most long-term investors rarely need but sometimes find valuable."],
  [37,"NAV","/ɛn-eɪ-viː/","noun","Net Asset Value — the per-share value of a fund's assets minus its liabilities, calculated at the end of each trading day. For mutual funds, NAV is the price at which shares are bought and sold. For ETFs, NAV is the underlying value, while the market price may trade at a slight premium or discount to NAV.","field","NAV is the accounting value of a fund share. For ETFs, the small premium or discount to NAV is usually arbitraged away quickly by institutional traders. A persistent large premium or discount — most commonly seen in closed-end funds — signals that supply and demand for the fund shares has decoupled from the underlying value of its holdings."],
  [38,"Open-End Fund","/ˈoʊpən-ɛnd fʌnd/","noun","A mutual fund that continuously issues and redeems shares at NAV. There is no fixed number of shares — new investors add capital and receive new shares; redeeming investors receive cash and their shares are retired. Open-end funds are priced once daily, after market close.","course","Open-end funds are the traditional mutual fund structure — familiar, liquid at NAV, and priced daily. The unlimited share issuance means the fund can grow indefinitely, but large redemptions can force the manager to sell holdings at inopportune times to meet cash needs. This is the structural tension that ETFs largely resolved through the in-kind mechanism."],
  [39,"Closed-End Fund","/kloʊzd-ɛnd fʌnd/","noun","An investment fund that raises a fixed amount of capital through an initial public offering, issues a set number of shares, and then trades on an exchange like a stock. Unlike open-end funds, the manager does not issue or redeem shares — buyers and sellers transact with each other in the secondary market.","field","Closed-end funds trade at prices determined by supply and demand, which may be above (premium) or below (discount) the NAV of their underlying holdings. A persistent discount represents an opportunity — buying a dollar of assets for less than a dollar — but discounts can persist indefinitely or widen further. Closed-end funds are more complex than they appear and reward careful analysis."],
  [40,"Interval Fund","/ˈɪntərvəl fʌnd/","noun","A type of fund that does not trade on an exchange but offers periodic — typically quarterly — liquidity windows during which investors can redeem shares, subject to limits. Interval funds often hold less liquid assets such as private credit, real estate, or alternatives that cannot be sold daily.","course","Interval funds provide access to illiquid asset classes in a regulated fund structure — bridging the gap between daily-liquid mutual funds and fully illiquid private investments. The tradeoff is restricted redemption: investors cannot access their capital at will. Understanding the redemption schedule and limits before investing is essential, particularly for assets that may be needed on a shorter timeline."],
  [41,"Target Date Fund","/ˈtɑːrɡɪt deɪt fʌnd/","noun","A fund designed for investors planning to retire or reach a financial goal in a specific year. It holds a diversified mix of equities and fixed income that automatically shifts toward more conservative allocations as the target date approaches, following a predetermined glide path.","field","Target date funds deliver automated asset allocation — removing the ongoing decision about how to adjust the mix over time. Their convenience is real. The limitation is that a single glide path cannot account for an individual investor's specific risk tolerance, other assets, or retirement income needs. They are a sensible default, not a personalized plan."],
  [42,"Glide Path","/ɡlaɪd pæθ/","noun","The predetermined schedule by which a target date fund — or an investor's overall portfolio — shifts from a more aggressive allocation to a more conservative one over time, typically reducing equity exposure and increasing fixed income as retirement approaches.","course","The glide path embeds a key assumption: that investors need less risk as they approach and enter retirement. The debate in the field is whether the path should reach its most conservative point at the target date or continue adjusting through retirement. A 'to' glide path reaches its endpoint at retirement; a 'through' glide path continues beyond it, assuming continued long-term investment needs."],
  [43,"Model Portfolio","/ˈmɒdəl pɔːrtˈfoʊlioʊ/","noun","A pre-constructed, standardized allocation across asset classes or individual securities, designed to be replicated across many client accounts simultaneously. Model portfolios allow advisors to implement consistent investment strategies efficiently and make changes to all accounts at once when the model is updated.","field","Model portfolios shift investment management from one-off decisions to systematic strategy. They bring consistency, scalability, and clear accountability for investment decisions. For clients, a model portfolio means their allocation is professionally designed and regularly maintained — not dependent on advisor capacity or attention on any given day."],
  [44,"Separately Managed Account","/ˈsɛpərɪtli ˈmænɪdʒd əˈkaʊnt/","noun","An investment account in which a professional manager directly owns individual securities on behalf of a single investor, rather than pooling assets in a fund. The investor holds the actual stocks or bonds — not shares of a fund — and can customize the portfolio for tax purposes or to exclude specific holdings.","course","Separately managed accounts offer a degree of personalization and tax management that pooled funds cannot match. The investor's cost basis in each security is their own, enabling precise tax-loss harvesting and avoiding the embedded gains that can accompany mutual fund purchases. The tradeoff is a typically higher investment minimum and fee structure."],
  [45,"Unified Managed Account","/ˈjuːnɪfaɪd ˈmænɪdʒd əˈkaʊnt/","noun","A single account structure that holds multiple investment strategies — model portfolios, separately managed accounts, ETFs, mutual funds — in a consolidated platform, managed under a coordinated tax and rebalancing overlay. The UMA provides a holistic view and coordinated management across asset classes and managers.","field","A unified managed account is the infrastructure of sophisticated portfolio management — one account, one statement, one tax overlay, multiple strategies. It eliminates the inefficiency of managing tax and rebalancing decisions across multiple separate accounts in isolation. For clients with complex portfolios, the coordination benefit is meaningful."],
  [46,"Tactical vs Strategic Allocation","/ˈtæktɪkəl vɜːrsəs strəˈtiːdʒɪk ˌæləˈkeɪʃən/","noun","Two approaches to portfolio construction. Strategic allocation sets a long-term target mix based on goals and risk tolerance and maintains it through rebalancing. Tactical allocation actively shifts the mix in response to near-term market conditions or economic views, deviating from the long-term target opportunistically.","course","Strategic allocation is the plan; tactical is the deviation from the plan. The case for tactical allocation is that markets are predictably mispriced in ways that can be exploited. The evidence for this is mixed — most tactical shifts add cost and behavioral risk without reliably adding return. A clear strategic allocation, consistently maintained, outperforms most tactical approaches over time."],
  [47,"Dollar-Cost Averaging","/ˈdɒlər-kɒst ˈævərɪdʒɪŋ/","noun","An investment strategy of investing a fixed dollar amount at regular intervals — monthly, quarterly — regardless of market price. When prices are lower, the fixed amount buys more shares; when prices are higher, it buys fewer. Over time, the average cost per share is lower than the average price.","field","Dollar-cost averaging is the mechanical implementation of investing discipline. It removes the timing decision — and the timing anxiety — from the process. The evidence suggests lump-sum investing outperforms it on average when a sum is available, because markets rise more often than they fall. But for regular contributions from income, DCA is the natural and effective default.","To invest a fixed dollar amount at regular intervals regardless of market price, automatically buying more shares when prices are low and fewer when prices are high. An investor who dollar-cost averages is removing the timing decision from the process and letting price variability work in their favor over time.","course","To dollar-cost average is to systematize the discipline. The fixed amount goes in on schedule — falling markets buy more shares at lower prices, rising markets buy fewer at higher ones. The averaging effect is mechanical and requires no forecasting. For regular contributions from income, it is the most reliable investment behavior available."],
  [48,"Turnover Rate","/ˈtɜːrnoʊvər reɪt/","noun","A measure of how frequently a fund buys and sells its holdings during a year, expressed as a percentage of the fund's average assets. A fund with 100 percent turnover has replaced its entire portfolio once during the year. High turnover generates transaction costs and, in taxable accounts, capital gains distributions.","course","Turnover rate is a window into management style and tax efficiency. Low-turnover funds tend to be more tax-efficient in taxable accounts and carry lower implicit trading costs. High-turnover funds may be pursuing strategies that require frequent repositioning — with the associated cost drag. For taxable investors, low turnover is an underappreciated component of after-tax return."],
  [49,"Tax Efficiency","/tæks ɪˈfɪʃənsi/","noun","The degree to which an investment minimizes taxable events — capital gains distributions, dividend income, interest income — relative to its total return. Tax-efficient investments deliver more of their return in forms that are either untaxed, deferred, or taxed at favorable rates.","field","Tax efficiency is a return component that most performance reports don't show. Two funds with identical pre-tax returns can deliver meaningfully different after-tax results based on how and when they generate taxable events. For assets held in taxable accounts, tax efficiency is as important as expense ratio in evaluating total cost."],
  [50,"Prospectus","/prəˈspɛktəs/","noun","A formal legal document required by the SEC for publicly offered securities and funds, disclosing material information about the investment — its objectives, strategies, risks, fees, and historical performance. Mutual fund and ETF prospectuses are filed with regulators and must be provided to investors before or at the time of purchase.","course","The prospectus is the authoritative disclosure document — dense by design, because it must cover all material information. Most investors never read it. They should at least read the summary prospectus, which distills the key facts: what the fund does, what it costs, and what risks it carries. The fees section alone is worth finding."],
  [51,"MLPs","/ɛm-ɛl-piːz/","noun","Master Limited Partnerships — publicly traded partnerships that combine the liquidity of a publicly traded security with the tax treatment of a partnership. MLPs are common in energy infrastructure — pipelines, storage, processing — and are structured to pass income through to unitholders with favorable tax treatment.","field","MLPs generate K-1 tax forms rather than 1099s, which adds complexity to tax preparation. Their income has historically carried a return-of-capital component that defers taxation, which benefits taxable investors but creates complications for tax-exempt entities. They are efficient in taxable accounts, inefficient in IRAs where the K-1 income can trigger unrelated business taxable income."],
  [52,"BDCs","/biː-diː-siːz/","noun","Business Development Companies — publicly traded investment funds that lend to or invest in small and mid-sized private companies. BDCs are required to distribute at least 90 percent of their taxable income to shareholders, resulting in high dividend yields. They provide retail investors access to private credit markets.","course","BDCs democratize access to private lending — an asset class previously available only to institutional investors. Their high yields reflect the credit risk of lending to smaller, often leveraged companies. In recessions, BDC portfolios can experience significant credit losses. They are income instruments with meaningful credit risk, not simply high-yield savings alternatives."],
  [53,"Commodities","/kəˈmɒdɪtiz/","noun","Physical goods traded in standardized contracts — agricultural products like corn, wheat, and soybeans; energy products like oil and natural gas; and metals like gold, silver, and copper. Commodity prices are driven by supply and demand dynamics distinct from those affecting financial assets.","field","Commodities add a dimension to a portfolio that equities and bonds cannot replicate: direct exposure to physical supply and demand. Their low correlation to financial assets and inflation-sensitive behavior make them a diversifier in certain environments. However, commodities generate no income and have produced modest returns over long periods — they are tools for specific purposes, not core holdings for most investors."],
  [54,"Futures","/ˈfjuːtʃərz/","noun","Standardized contracts obligating the buyer to purchase, and the seller to deliver, a specified quantity of an asset at a defined price on a future date. Futures trade on exchanges and are used by producers to hedge price risk and by investors to gain leveraged exposure to asset prices.","course","Futures are the mechanism through which most commodity exposure is accessed in investment portfolios — physical delivery of oil or corn is impractical for most investors. Commodity futures carry a cost called roll yield, which can be negative when the futures curve is in contango, meaning future prices are higher than spot prices. Understanding roll yield is essential to understanding why commodity index returns often lag spot price movements."],
  [55,"Options","/ˈɒpʃənz/","noun","Contracts that give the buyer the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell an underlying security at a specified price — the strike price — on or before a specified date. Options can be used to hedge existing positions, generate income, or speculate on price movements.","field","Options are simultaneously one of the most versatile and most misunderstood tools in investing. They allow precise expression of views on direction, magnitude, and timing of price movements. Used for hedging, they reduce risk. Used speculatively with leverage, they can produce rapid and total loss of the premium paid. The same instrument serves very different purposes depending on how it is applied."],
  [56,"Calls","/kɔːlz/","noun","Options contracts that give the buyer the right to purchase the underlying security at the strike price before expiration. The buyer pays a premium for this right. Call buyers profit when the underlying security rises above the strike price plus the premium paid.","course","A call is a leveraged bet on appreciation — the buyer controls a large position for a fraction of the cost of owning it outright. If the stock rises above the strike, the call gains value. If it doesn't, the entire premium is lost. The asymmetry is attractive to buyers and is what makes selling calls an income strategy for those who already own the underlying."],
  [57,"Puts","/pʊts/","noun","Options contracts that give the buyer the right to sell the underlying security at the strike price before expiration. The buyer pays a premium for this right. Put buyers profit when the underlying security falls below the strike price minus the premium paid.","field","A put is a leveraged bet on decline — or a hedge against decline in a position already owned. Buying puts on a portfolio holding is insurance: the cost is the premium, the benefit is protection against a significant drop. Like all insurance, puts have a cost that erodes returns when the protection isn't needed — which is most of the time."],
  [58,"Strike Price","/straɪk praɪs/","noun","The predetermined price at which an option contract can be exercised — the price at which the underlying security can be bought (call) or sold (put). The relationship between the strike price and the current market price determines whether an option is in the money, at the money, or out of the money.","course","The strike price is the anchor of an option's value. An option is in the money when it would be profitable to exercise immediately — the stock is above the call's strike or below the put's strike. At the money is right at the current price. Out of the money has no intrinsic value yet. These distinctions affect the premium paid and the probability of a profitable outcome."],
  [59,"Expiration","/ˌɛkspɪˈreɪʃən/","noun","The date on which an options contract becomes void and can no longer be exercised. Options lose their time value as expiration approaches — a phenomenon called time decay or theta. At expiration, an option is worth only its intrinsic value, if any, or nothing at all.","field","Expiration creates urgency that doesn't exist in stock ownership. An option that is out of the money at expiration expires worthless — the entire premium is gone. The time dimension of options is both their leverage mechanism and their most significant risk. Longer expiration provides more time for the thesis to play out; shorter expiration is less expensive but more unforgiving."],
  [60,"Covered Call","/ˈkʌvərd kɔːl/","noun","An options strategy in which an investor who owns shares of a stock sells call options on those shares. The premium received from selling the call provides income and limited downside cushion. If the stock rises above the strike price, the shares may be called away — sold at the strike — capping the upside.","course","The covered call is the most commonly used options strategy among individual investors because it generates income from an existing position. The tradeoff is capped upside — if the stock surges, the owner participates only to the strike price. It is an income strategy, not a growth strategy, and is most effective on positions where significant appreciation is not expected.","To sell call options against a stock position already owned, collecting a premium in exchange for accepting a ceiling on the position's upside. An investor who writes a covered call is monetizing the right to buy their shares at a higher price — receiving income now in exchange for capping how much they participate if the stock surges.","field","To write a covered call is to accept a bounded outcome in exchange for immediate cash. The premium arrives regardless of what happens. If the stock stays flat or falls modestly, the call expires worthless and the premium is pure gain. If the stock surges past the strike, the shares get called away at the agreed price — the investor keeps the premium but misses the upside above the strike."],
  [61,"Protective Put","/prəˈtɛktɪv pʊt/","noun","An options strategy in which an investor buys put options on shares they own, creating a floor below which their loss cannot extend. The put acts as insurance — the owner pays a premium in exchange for the right to sell at the strike price regardless of how far the stock falls.","field","A protective put is portfolio insurance with a defined cost. Like homeowners insurance, you pay whether or not you need it — and most of the time you don't need it. The discipline is accepting that cost as the price of protection during periods of significant uncertainty. For concentrated positions or large holdings near important price levels, it is a rational risk management tool."],
  [62,"Collar","/ˈkɒlər/","noun","An options strategy combining a protective put and a covered call on the same underlying position. The investor buys a put to establish a downside floor and sells a call to finance the cost of the put, accepting a ceiling on upside in exchange. A zero-cost collar sets strike prices such that the premium received from the call equals the cost of the put.","course","A collar is a bounded position — loss is limited, gain is limited. It is frequently used to protect a large concentrated stock position without selling it, which would trigger taxes. The zero-cost version is particularly useful when volatility is high and option premiums are elevated, making the financing efficient. The cost is giving up the upside above the call strike.","To establish a bounded position by simultaneously buying a put and selling a call on the same underlying holding, creating both a floor and a ceiling. An investor who collars a position accepts a defined range of outcomes in exchange for protection against catastrophic loss — often without paying out of pocket if the options are structured to offset each other.","field","To collar a concentrated position is to contain it without selling it. The put limits the downside; the call finances the put and caps the upside. The position can no longer lose catastrophically, but it also cannot gain beyond the call strike. For a large, appreciated holding where selling would trigger significant taxes, collaring buys time and protection without triggering the tax event."],
  [63,"Warrants","/ˈwɔːrənts/","noun","Securities issued by a company that give the holder the right to purchase shares at a specified price — the exercise price — within a defined period. Unlike options, which are contracts between investors, warrants are issued directly by the company and result in the issuance of new shares when exercised.","field","Warrants are long-dated call options issued by the company itself. They are common in special purpose acquisition companies, private placements, and some corporate financing structures. When exercised, they dilute existing shareholders because new shares are created. The dilutive effect distinguishes them from options on secondary market shares, where exercise simply transfers existing shares."],
  [64,"Rights Offerings","/raɪts ˈɔːfərɪŋz/","noun","A corporate action that gives existing shareholders the opportunity to purchase additional shares at a discounted price before new shares are offered to the public. Rights are proportional to current holdings and are typically transferable — shareholders who don't wish to participate can sell their rights.","course","A rights offering is a capital-raising mechanism that respects the existing ownership structure by giving current shareholders first access. Shareholders who exercise their rights maintain their proportional ownership. Those who don't — and don't sell their rights — are diluted. The discounted price makes the rights themselves valuable, which is why selling them is a legitimate alternative to exercising them."],
  [65,"ADR","/eɪ-diː-ɑːr/","noun","American Depositary Receipt — a negotiable certificate issued by a U.S. bank representing shares of a foreign company's stock. ADRs trade on U.S. exchanges in U.S. dollars, allowing American investors to hold foreign company exposure without navigating foreign exchanges, currencies, or settlement systems.","field","ADRs make international investing accessible through familiar infrastructure. The investor holds a receipt representing foreign shares, with the underlying shares held by a depositary bank. Dividends are converted to dollars before distribution. The currency exposure remains — the ADR price still reflects both the company's performance and the exchange rate between the dollar and the foreign currency."],
  [66,"Currency Risk","/ˈkʌrənsi rɪsk/","noun","The risk that changes in exchange rates will affect the value of foreign investments when converted back to the investor's home currency. A foreign investment that performs well in local currency terms can still produce a loss for a U.S. investor if the foreign currency weakens against the dollar.","course","Currency risk is the invisible layer in international investing. An investor who bought European stocks in euros several years ago may have experienced strong local returns — and seen them partially or fully offset by euro depreciation against the dollar. Currency-hedged funds eliminate this risk at a cost; unhedged funds accept it as part of the international exposure."],
  [67,"Emerging Markets","/ɪˈmɜːrdʒɪŋ ˈmɑːrkɪts/","noun","Countries in the process of rapid economic development, characterized by growing but less mature capital markets, higher growth potential, and higher risk than developed markets. Common classifications include Brazil, China, India, Mexico, and South Africa, though the definition varies by index provider.","field","Emerging markets offer growth exposure that developed markets cannot replicate — populations expanding, middle classes forming, infrastructure being built. The risks are commensurately higher: currency volatility, political instability, regulatory unpredictability, and less investor protection. For long-term investors, the question is not whether to include them but how much, and through what vehicle."],
  [68,"Margin","/ˈmɑːrdʒɪn/","noun","Borrowed money from a brokerage used to purchase securities, using the account's existing holdings as collateral. Margin amplifies both gains and losses — a 10 percent gain on a fully margined position produces a 20 percent return on the investor's equity; a 10 percent loss produces a 20 percent loss. A margin call occurs when account equity falls below the required minimum.","course","Margin introduces leverage into equity investing — and leverage is a multiplier in both directions. The math of losses on margin is particularly unforgiving: a 50 percent decline in a fully margined position wipes out equity entirely. Margin calls can force the liquidation of positions at the worst possible time. For most investors, margin represents a risk that the potential return does not adequately compensate.","To borrow funds from a brokerage against an existing account's holdings in order to purchase additional securities. An investor who buys on margin is amplifying their market exposure — and their risk — beyond what their own capital would permit.","course","To margin a position is to multiply the stakes. Gains are amplified; losses are equally amplified. The brokerage's interest meter runs continuously, and a margin call can force selling at the worst possible moment — when the market has moved against the position and the account's equity has fallen below the required threshold. For most long-term investors, the leverage margin provides is not worth what it costs when markets turn."],
  [69,"Short Selling","/ʃɔːrt ˈsɛlɪŋ/","noun","The practice of borrowing shares of a security, selling them in the open market, and later repurchasing them to return to the lender — with the intent of profiting from a price decline. The short seller profits if the price falls and loses if it rises. Potential losses are theoretically unlimited if the price rises without bound.","field","Short selling is directionally opposite to all other investing — the investor profits from decline rather than appreciation. It requires borrowing shares, paying a borrowing cost, and accepting theoretically unlimited loss risk if the position moves adversely. It is a legitimate risk management and price discovery mechanism, but one that demands both conviction and careful risk management.","To borrow shares of a security and sell them in the open market with the intention of repurchasing them later at a lower price, profiting from the decline. An investor who shorts a stock is making a directional bet against it — and accepting that losses are theoretically unlimited if the price rises instead of falls.","course","To short a stock is to invert the normal investor's posture. Instead of buying and hoping for appreciation, the short seller borrows, sells, and hopes for decline. The profit is the difference between the sale price and the repurchase price — but the risk is asymmetric. A stock can only fall to zero; it can rise without limit. Every day the position is open, the clock and the borrowing cost both run."],
  [70,"Alpha","/ˈælfə/","noun","The excess return of an investment relative to its benchmark, after adjusting for the market risk taken. Positive alpha indicates the investment outperformed what its risk level would have predicted; negative alpha indicates underperformance. Alpha is the measure of genuine skill-based value added by active management.","course","Alpha is what active managers are paid to generate — and what most fail to produce consistently after fees. Finding genuine, persistent alpha requires either superior information, superior analysis, or structural advantages that most participants do not possess. The difficulty of producing alpha is the primary argument for passive investing: if the expected alpha is zero or negative after fees, why pay for active management?"],
  [71,"Beta","/ˈbeɪtə/","noun","A measure of an investment's sensitivity to market movements relative to a benchmark. A beta of 1 means the investment moves in line with the market. A beta greater than 1 indicates higher volatility than the market — it amplifies market moves in both directions. A beta less than 1 indicates lower volatility. Negative beta indicates inverse movement.","field","Beta measures systematic risk — the risk that cannot be diversified away. It tells you how much market exposure a security or portfolio carries. High-beta positions amplify market swings; low-beta positions dampen them. Managing portfolio beta is how investors adjust overall market sensitivity without completely changing asset allocation."],
  [72,"Sharpe Ratio","/ʃɑːrp ˈreɪʃioʊ/","noun","A measure of risk-adjusted return, calculated by dividing the excess return of an investment above the risk-free rate by its standard deviation. A higher Sharpe ratio indicates better return per unit of risk taken. It allows comparison of investments with different return levels and volatility profiles.","course","The Sharpe ratio answers the question: how much return are you getting for the risk you're taking? A high-return investment with very high volatility may have a lower Sharpe ratio than a moderate-return investment with low volatility — meaning the moderate investment is delivering more reward per unit of risk. It is the most widely used single metric for comparing risk-adjusted performance."],
  [73,"Sortino Ratio","/sɔːrˈtiːnoʊ ˈreɪʃioʊ/","noun","A variation of the Sharpe ratio that measures risk-adjusted return using only downside volatility — the standard deviation of negative returns — rather than total volatility. It penalizes only the harmful kind of volatility, on the theory that upside variability is not a risk to be minimized.","field","The Sortino ratio is the Sharpe ratio's more discerning companion. If two investments have the same average return and the same total volatility, but one achieves it through consistent gains with occasional dips while the other achieves it through wild swings in both directions, the Sortino ratio correctly identifies the first as more desirable. It is particularly relevant for evaluating strategies where preserving capital on the downside is the primary concern."]
];


const taxesTerms = [
  [1,"Ordinary Income","/ˈɔːrdɪnɛri ˈɪnkʌm/","noun","Income taxed at standard federal income tax rates — wages, salaries, self-employment income, interest, and short-term capital gains. Ordinary income sits at the top of the tax hierarchy and is taxed at the highest applicable rate for the taxpayer's bracket.","field","Understanding what qualifies as ordinary income clarifies why investment structure matters. Interest income is ordinary. A salary is ordinary. But long-term capital gains and qualified dividends are not — they receive preferential rates. The distinction shapes how income-generating assets are best held and managed."],
  [2,"Marginal Rate","/ˈmɑːrdʒɪnəl reɪt/","noun","The tax rate applied to the last dollar of taxable income — the rate at the top of the taxpayer's current bracket. The marginal rate is not the rate applied to all income, only to income within and above the applicable bracket threshold.","course","The marginal rate is the rate that matters for planning decisions at the margin: whether to make a Roth conversion, take a deduction, or recognize a gain. It answers the question: what does one more dollar of income actually cost in taxes this year?"],
  [3,"Effective Rate","/ɪˈfɛktɪv reɪt/","noun","The average rate at which a taxpayer's total income is taxed, calculated by dividing total tax paid by total taxable income. The effective rate is always lower than the marginal rate because lower brackets apply to the first layers of income.","field","The effective rate tells you what you actually paid as a percentage of income. It is the honest summary of the tax burden — useful for comparison, budgeting, and communicating the overall tax picture. The marginal rate tells you what the next dollar costs; the effective rate tells you what the whole year cost."],
  [4,"FICA","/ˈfaɪkə/","noun","Federal Insurance Contributions Act taxes — the payroll taxes that fund Social Security and Medicare. For employees, FICA is split between the employer and employee, each paying 7.65 percent of wages up to the Social Security wage base, with the Medicare portion applying to all wages without a cap.","course","FICA is often invisible because it is withheld before the paycheck is seen — but it is a meaningful tax on earned income. Self-employed individuals pay both the employer and employee portions, making self-employment tax one of the most significant tax considerations for independent workers."],
  [5,"Self-Employment Tax","/sɛlf-ɪmˈplɔɪmənt tæks/","noun","The tax equivalent of FICA for self-employed individuals, covering both the employer and employee portions of Social Security and Medicare. The self-employment tax rate is 15.3 percent on net self-employment income up to the Social Security wage base, with 2.9 percent continuing above that threshold for Medicare. Half of the self-employment tax is deductible as an above-the-line deduction.","field","Self-employment tax arrives as a surprise to many first-time freelancers and business owners. The combined 15.3 percent on top of income tax can push the effective marginal rate significantly higher than expected. The deduction for half of SE tax softens the impact but does not eliminate it — planning for quarterly estimated payments is essential."],
  [6,"Estimated Quarterly Taxes","/ˈɛstɪmeɪtɪd ˈkwɔːrtərli ˈtæksɪz/","noun","Payments made to the IRS four times per year by taxpayers whose income is not subject to withholding — self-employed individuals, investors with significant unearned income, and others with tax liability not covered by employer withholding. The payments are due in April, June, September, and January.","course","Estimated payments replace the withholding system for income that doesn't flow through an employer. Missing them or underpaying triggers an underpayment penalty — not a dramatic one, but avoidable. The safe harbor calculation — paying either 100 percent of last year's tax liability or 90 percent of this year's — provides a reliable target without requiring precise income forecasting."],
  [7,"Safe Harbor","/seɪf ˈhɑːrbər/","noun","A provision that protects a taxpayer from underpayment penalties if they meet specific payment thresholds. For estimated taxes, safe harbor is generally met by paying either 100 percent of the prior year's tax liability (110 percent for high earners) or 90 percent of the current year's actual tax liability, whichever is smaller.","field","Safe harbor converts the uncertainty of income forecasting into a manageable target. Rather than trying to project this year's exact liability — which may be unknowable early in the year — paying to the prior-year safe harbor threshold ensures penalty protection regardless of what the final number turns out to be."],
  [8,"Underpayment Penalty","/ˌʌndərˈpeɪmənt ˈpɛnəlti/","noun","A penalty assessed by the IRS when a taxpayer fails to pay enough tax through withholding or estimated payments during the year. The penalty is calculated as interest on the shortfall for the period it remained unpaid — modest in absolute terms but avoidable through timely estimated payments or safe harbor compliance.","course","The underpayment penalty is not a punishment — it is essentially an interest charge for using money that belonged to the government. For most taxpayers, the goal is simply to meet the safe harbor threshold consistently and avoid the administrative friction of calculating and disputing the penalty."],
  [9,"SALT Deduction","/sɒlt dɪˈdʌkʃən/","noun","The State and Local Tax deduction — an itemized deduction for state and local income taxes (or sales taxes) and property taxes paid during the tax year. Since 2018, the deduction has been capped at $10,000 per return, a significant limitation for taxpayers in high-tax states.","field","The $10,000 SALT cap changed the math of itemizing for millions of homeowners in high-tax states. Property taxes and state income taxes that once pushed itemized deductions well above the standard deduction now frequently don't — making the standard deduction the better choice for households that previously itemized routinely. The cap is scheduled to expire and has been subject to ongoing legislative discussion."],
  [10,"AMT","/eɪ-ɛm-tiː/","noun","Alternative Minimum Tax — a parallel tax system with its own set of rules, rates, and exemptions designed to ensure that high-income taxpayers pay a minimum level of tax regardless of deductions and credits used under the regular system. Taxpayers calculate tax under both systems and pay whichever is higher.","course","The AMT was originally designed to prevent high earners from using legitimate deductions to eliminate their tax liability entirely. For most middle-income taxpayers, the inflation-adjusted exemption keeps them out of AMT territory. For those with significant incentive stock options, high state and local taxes, or large miscellaneous deductions, AMT awareness is essential."],
  [11,"Qualified Dividends","/ˈkwɒlɪfaɪd ˈdɪvɪdɛndz/","noun","Dividends paid by U.S. corporations or qualifying foreign corporations that meet IRS holding period requirements. Qualified dividends are taxed at the lower long-term capital gains rates — 0, 15, or 20 percent — rather than ordinary income rates. Ordinary dividends that do not meet the requirements are taxed as ordinary income.","field","The distinction between qualified and ordinary dividends can be significant at higher income levels. Most dividends from U.S. stocks held in standard brokerage accounts qualify. REITs, money market funds, and certain foreign dividends typically do not. The 1099-DIV received each year separates qualified from ordinary dividends."],
  [12,"Long-Term Capital Gains","/lɒŋ-tɜːrm ˈkæpɪtəl ɡeɪnz/","noun","Gains from the sale of assets held for more than one year, taxed at preferential rates of 0, 15, or 20 percent depending on taxable income. The holding period is measured from the day after acquisition to the day of sale.","course","The one-year threshold is one of the most valuable lines in the tax code. Crossing it converts a gain taxed at ordinary rates into one taxed at significantly lower preferential rates. For investors with flexibility in timing, holding past the one-year mark is often the single most impactful tax decision available."],
  [13,"Short-Term Capital Gains","/ʃɔːrt-tɜːrm ˈkæpɪtəl ɡeɪnz/","noun","Gains from the sale of assets held for one year or less, taxed as ordinary income at the taxpayer's applicable marginal rate. Short-term gains receive no preferential treatment — they are taxed identically to wages.","field","Short-term gains are expensive. Selling a position less than a year after purchase can nearly double the tax cost compared to waiting. When markets move quickly and the temptation to sell is strong, the tax calendar is a meaningful input to the decision — not the only one, but not a trivial one either."],
  [14,"Tax Basis","/tæks ˈbeɪsɪs/","noun","The original cost or value of an asset used to calculate gain or loss upon sale. For most purchased assets, basis equals the purchase price plus transaction costs. Basis determines how much of the sale proceeds is return of capital and how much is taxable gain.","course","Tax basis is the starting line for every capital gains calculation. Without knowing basis, the gain or loss from a sale cannot be determined. Tracking basis carefully — especially for assets acquired in multiple lots, through inheritance, or as gifts — prevents both overpaying taxes and understating them."],
  [15,"Cost Basis Methods","/kɒst ˈbeɪsɪs ˈmɛθədz/","noun","The accounting methods used to identify which specific shares or units are being sold when a position was acquired in multiple purchases at different prices. Common methods include FIFO (first in, first out), specific identification, and average cost. The method chosen affects the size of the gain or loss recognized.","field","Cost basis method selection is a lever that most investors never pull — but it can meaningfully affect the tax outcome of a sale. Specific identification allows the highest-basis shares to be sold first, minimizing the gain. FIFO sells the oldest shares first, which may produce larger gains in a rising market. The choice must be made at the time of sale, not retroactively."],
  [16,"FIFO","/ˈfaɪfoʊ/","noun","First In, First Out — a cost basis accounting method that assumes the oldest shares or units in a position are sold first. In a rising market, FIFO typically produces larger taxable gains because the older shares have a lower cost basis than more recently acquired ones.","course","FIFO is the default for most brokerages if no other method is specified. In a long-held, appreciated position, FIFO can produce a very large gain on a partial sale — potentially more than specific identification would. Knowing the default matters most when you intend something different."],
  [17,"Specific Identification","/spɪˈsɪfɪk aɪˌdɛntɪfɪˈkeɪʃən/","noun","A cost basis method that allows the taxpayer to designate exactly which shares or units are being sold, enabling the selection of highest-basis lots to minimize taxable gain or maximize a loss. Requires proper identification at the time of sale and adequate recordkeeping.","field","Specific identification is the most tax-efficient basis method for most investors. By selling the shares with the highest cost basis first, gains are minimized and losses are maximized. It requires intentionality — the identification must be made at the time of sale, not after — but the tax savings over time can be meaningful."],
  [18,"Carryover Basis","/ˈkæriˌoʊvər ˈbeɪsɪs/","noun","A basis rule that applies when property is received as a gift — the recipient inherits the donor's original cost basis rather than the fair market value at the time of the gift. If the recipient later sells the property, gain is measured from the donor's original basis, which may be much lower than the current value.","course","Carryover basis preserves the embedded gain from the donor's perspective — the gift transfers the asset but not the tax obligation. In contrast, property transferred at death typically receives a step-up in basis. The distinction is significant for estate planning: gifting appreciated assets can shift a large tax bill to the recipient, while leaving them to be inherited may eliminate it entirely."],
  [19,"Step-Up in Basis","/stɛp-ʌp ɪn ˈbeɪsɪs/","noun","A tax provision that resets the cost basis of inherited assets to their fair market value on the date of the original owner's death. The result is that any appreciation that occurred during the decedent's lifetime is never subject to capital gains tax — the heir's basis begins at the current value.","field","Step-up in basis is one of the most powerful and underappreciated provisions in the tax code. It effectively forgives a lifetime of capital gains at death. For appreciated assets — a family home, a long-held stock portfolio, farmland — the tax savings can be enormous. Estate planning that accounts for step-up can preserve significantly more wealth for heirs than strategies that transfer appreciated assets during life."],
  [20,"Wash Sale Rule","/wɒʃ seɪl ruːl/","noun","An IRS rule that disallows a capital loss deduction if the taxpayer purchases a substantially identical security within 30 days before or after the sale that generated the loss. The disallowed loss is added to the basis of the replacement security, deferring rather than permanently eliminating it.","course","The wash sale rule is triggered by the 61-day window — 30 days before the sale, the day of, and 30 days after. Selling at a loss and immediately repurchasing the same security to capture the tax benefit does not work. The loss is deferred, not lost — but it doesn't help the current year's tax bill. Replacement with a similar but not substantially identical security preserves the loss."],
  [21,"Tax-Loss Harvesting","/tæks-lɒs ˈhɑːrvɪstɪŋ/","noun","The practice of intentionally selling securities at a loss to offset capital gains realized elsewhere in the portfolio, reducing the overall tax liability for the year. Losses can offset gains dollar for dollar; excess losses can offset up to $3,000 of ordinary income annually, with remaining losses carried forward.","field","Tax-loss harvesting converts an underperforming position into a tax asset without necessarily changing the portfolio's overall market exposure — a similar security can be purchased to maintain the investment profile while observing the wash sale window. Over time, consistent harvesting compounds in meaningful ways, particularly in larger taxable portfolios.","To intentionally sell securities at a loss in order to offset realized capital gains and reduce current-year tax liability. A taxpayer who harvests losses is converting portfolio underperformance into a tax asset, without necessarily changing their market exposure if a replacement security is purchased.","course","To harvest a tax loss is to find the silver lining in a declining position. The sale captures the loss for tax purposes; a replacement security maintains market exposure. The wash sale window governs the timing — the replacement must be meaningfully different to preserve the deduction. Done consistently, harvesting builds a carried-loss reserve that compounds its value over time."],
  [22,"Tax Diversification","/tæks ˌdaɪvɜːrsɪfɪˈkeɪʃən/","noun","A retirement and investment planning strategy that distributes assets across accounts with different tax treatments — pre-tax, Roth, and taxable — to provide flexibility in managing taxable income in retirement. Having assets in all three categories allows withdrawals to be sourced strategically based on the tax environment at the time.","course","Tax diversification is the retirement version of not putting all eggs in one basket — applied to tax treatment rather than asset class. No one knows what tax rates will look like in 20 or 30 years. Having meaningful balances in pre-tax, Roth, and taxable accounts allows the retiree to draw from whichever bucket is most efficient in a given year."],
  [23,"Tax-Equivalent Yield","/tæks-ɪˈkwɪvələnt jiːld/","noun","A calculation that converts the yield of a tax-exempt investment — typically a municipal bond — into the equivalent pre-tax yield needed from a taxable investment to produce the same after-tax return. Formula: tax-exempt yield divided by (1 minus the marginal tax rate).","field","Tax-equivalent yield makes municipal bonds comparable to taxable bonds on an apples-to-apples basis. A 3 percent municipal bond yield for a taxpayer in the 37 percent bracket is equivalent to approximately 4.76 percent taxable. Whether munis make sense depends entirely on the taxpayer's bracket — at lower rates, the calculation often favors taxable alternatives."],
  [24,"Municipal Bonds","/mjuːˈnɪsɪpəl bɒndz/","noun","Debt securities issued by state, local, and municipal governments to fund public projects. Interest income from most municipal bonds is exempt from federal income tax and, in many cases, from state and local taxes for residents of the issuing state. In exchange for the tax benefit, yields are typically lower than comparable taxable bonds.","course","Municipal bonds are a tax-efficiency tool, not merely a fixed income instrument. Their value increases proportionally with the investor's marginal tax rate — they are most beneficial for investors in higher brackets. For tax-deferred accounts, the tax advantage is redundant and taxable bonds typically offer better yields."],
  [25,"NIIT","/ɛn-aɪ-aɪ-tiː/","noun","Net Investment Income Tax — a 3.8 percent surtax on the lesser of net investment income or the amount by which modified adjusted gross income exceeds a threshold ($200,000 for single filers, $250,000 for married filing jointly as of current law). Investment income includes capital gains, dividends, interest, rental income, and passive activity income.","field","NIIT is effectively an additional layer of tax on investment income for higher earners — one that is easy to overlook because it arrives as a surcharge on top of regular capital gains and ordinary income taxes. Planning around the NIIT threshold — through timing of gains, Roth conversions, or strategic loss harvesting — can meaningfully reduce its impact."],
  [26,"Medicare Surtax","/ˈmɛdɪkɛr ˈsɜːrtæks/","noun","Often used interchangeably with NIIT, the Medicare surtax also refers to the additional 0.9 percent Medicare tax on earned income (wages and self-employment income) above the same thresholds that trigger NIIT. Together, the two surtaxes represent a meaningful additional tax burden on high earners from both investment and employment income.","course","The Medicare surtax on earned income and the NIIT on investment income together form a high-income surcharge layer that is frequently underestimated in planning projections. Year-end income management — deferring income, accelerating deductions, timing capital events — becomes especially valuable when these thresholds are in play."],
  [27,"Passive Activity Rules","/ˈpæsɪv ækˈtɪvɪti ruːlz/","noun","IRS rules that limit the deductibility of losses from passive activities — generally defined as business activities in which the taxpayer does not materially participate. Passive losses can only offset passive income; they cannot be used to reduce wages, salaries, or portfolio income, and are suspended until the passive activity is sold.","field","Passive activity rules exist to prevent high-income taxpayers from using paper losses from real estate or business investments to shelter ordinary income. Understanding whether an activity is passive or active — based on hours of participation and material involvement — determines whether losses are currently usable or must be carried forward."],
  [28,"At-Risk Rules","/æt-rɪsk ruːlz/","noun","Tax rules that limit the deductibility of losses from a business or investment activity to the amount the taxpayer has actually at risk — meaning invested capital and personally guaranteed debt. Losses that exceed the at-risk amount are suspended and carried forward until the taxpayer has sufficient at-risk basis.","course","At-risk rules apply before passive activity rules in the loss limitation hierarchy. Even if an activity generates a passive loss that could otherwise be used, the taxpayer can only deduct up to the amount they could actually lose. Non-recourse financing typically does not count as at-risk, with a specific exception for qualified real estate non-recourse debt."],
  [29,"Net Operating Loss","/nɛt ˈɒpəreɪtɪŋ lɒs/","noun","A net operating loss occurs when a taxpayer's allowable deductions exceed their gross income in a given tax year, resulting in negative taxable income. Under current rules, NOLs can be carried forward indefinitely to offset future taxable income, subject to an 80 percent of taxable income limitation in each carryforward year.","field","NOLs are most relevant for business owners and self-employed individuals who experience loss years — startup phases, downturns, or extraordinary expenses. A carried-forward NOL is a future tax asset: a pool of negative income that reduces taxable income in profitable years ahead. Tracking it carefully ensures it is used when available."],
  [30,"Bonus Depreciation","/ˈboʊnəs dɪˌpriːʃiˈeɪʃən/","noun","An accelerated depreciation method that allows businesses to immediately deduct a large percentage of the cost of qualifying property — equipment, machinery, certain improvements — in the year it is placed in service, rather than depreciating it over its useful life. The deductible percentage has varied by year and legislation.","course","Bonus depreciation can dramatically reduce taxable income in the year a major business asset is acquired. It is a timing tool — it accelerates deductions from future years into the current year, reducing taxes now at the cost of smaller deductions later. For businesses with strong current-year income, it is a powerful lever.","To apply accelerated first-year depreciation to a qualifying business asset, deducting a large portion of its cost in the year it is placed in service rather than spreading the deduction over its useful life. When a business bonus-depreciates an asset, it is pulling future deductions forward into the present.","field","To bonus-depreciate an asset is to front-load the tax benefit. The asset's full economic life unfolds over years — but the deduction arrives immediately, in the year of purchase. For a business with significant current-year income, that timing is the difference between a large tax bill and a manageable one. The tradeoff: smaller deductions in future years when they may be equally needed."],
  [31,"Section 179","/ˈsɛkʃən wʌn-ˈsɛvənti-naɪn/","noun","An IRS provision that allows businesses to deduct the full purchase price of qualifying equipment and property in the year of purchase, up to an annual dollar limit. Unlike bonus depreciation, Section 179 is limited to the business's taxable income — it cannot create or increase a net operating loss.","field","Section 179 and bonus depreciation often work together for business asset purchases, and the distinction matters: Section 179 is limited to the business's net income, while bonus depreciation can go below zero and create an NOL. For businesses with modest income, Section 179 is the more constrained tool — bonus depreciation provides more flexibility."],
  [32,"Phantom Income","/ˈfæntəm ˈɪnkʌm/","noun","Taxable income that a taxpayer must recognize and pay tax on even though they have not received the corresponding cash. Common in partnerships, S-corporations, and certain bond investments where the taxpayer is allocated income that remains in the entity or is accrued rather than distributed.","course","Phantom income creates a cash flow problem: the tax bill arrives before the money does. It is most commonly encountered by partners in profitable partnerships that retain earnings, holders of zero-coupon bonds accruing interest, and certain real estate investors. Understanding when phantom income applies — and planning for the resulting tax liability — prevents unpleasant surprises at filing."],
  [33,"K-1","/keɪ-wʌn/","noun","A tax form issued by partnerships, S-corporations, trusts, and estates to report each owner's or beneficiary's share of income, deductions, credits, and other tax items. The K-1 passes the entity's tax attributes through to the individual's personal return.","field","K-1s are the tax signature of pass-through entities — they arrive later than W-2s and 1099s, often requiring filing extensions, and they can contain a wide range of items that affect the individual return in complex ways. Investors in partnerships, private funds, or S-corporations should expect K-1s and plan accordingly for both the timing and complexity they introduce."],
  [34,"Foreign Tax Credit","/ˈfɒrən tæks ˈkrɛdɪt/","noun","A nonrefundable tax credit that reduces U.S. federal income tax dollar-for-dollar for income taxes paid to foreign governments on foreign-source income. It prevents the same income from being taxed twice — once by the foreign country and once by the U.S.","course","The foreign tax credit is relevant for any investor holding international funds or individual foreign securities. It appears automatically on Form 1099-DIV for most fund investors and reduces U.S. tax liability by the amount of foreign taxes already paid. Taking the credit is generally more beneficial than deducting the foreign taxes — a credit reduces tax directly, while a deduction only reduces taxable income."],
  [35,"Kiddie Tax","/ˈkɪdi tæks/","noun","A tax rule that applies the parents' marginal tax rate to a child's unearned income — investment income, dividends, capital gains — above a threshold amount. It applies to children under age 19, and full-time students under age 24, who have at least one living parent. It was designed to prevent high-income parents from shifting investment assets to children to take advantage of lower rates.","field","The Kiddie Tax largely eliminates the income-shifting benefit of UGMA and UTMA accounts for families in higher brackets. Once a child's unearned income exceeds the threshold, the excess is taxed at the parents' rate — not the child's. For significant investment accounts held for minors, the tax efficiency argument for custodial accounts weakens considerably for high-income families."],
  [36,"Trust Tax Rates","/trʌst tæks reɪts/","noun","The income tax rate schedule applicable to trusts and estates, which reaches the highest marginal bracket at a very low income threshold — significantly lower than the threshold for individual taxpayers. Trusts that accumulate income rather than distributing it to beneficiaries are subject to these compressed rates.","course","Trust tax rates compress quickly — the top bracket is reached at a fraction of the income that would trigger it for an individual. This makes income accumulation inside a trust tax-inefficient for most purposes. Distributing income to beneficiaries in lower brackets is typically the more tax-efficient approach, and it is a primary reason trust distributions are common in estate planning."],
  [37,"QCD","/kjuː-siː-diː/","noun","Qualified Charitable Distribution — a direct transfer of up to $105,000 annually (indexed for inflation) from a traditional IRA to a qualified charity, available to account holders age 70½ and older. The amount transferred is excluded from taxable income and counts toward the Required Minimum Distribution.","field","The QCD is among the most tax-efficient charitable giving strategies available to retirees with IRA assets. It satisfies the RMD without increasing taxable income — the distribution is invisible to the income calculation, which matters for Medicare premium thresholds, NIIT exposure, and the taxability of Social Security benefits. For charitably inclined retirees, it is almost always more efficient than taking the RMD, paying tax, and then donating the after-tax amount.","To make a Qualified Charitable Distribution — transferring funds directly from a traditional IRA to a qualified charity, bypassing the account holder's taxable income entirely. A retiree who QCDs satisfies all or part of their RMD obligation while keeping that income off their return.","course","To QCD is to give from the most tax-efficient source available. The money moves from the IRA directly to the charity — it never enters the account holder's income, never triggers IRMAA surcharges, never affects Social Security taxability. For a charitably inclined retiree with IRA assets, QCDing the RMD is almost always the cleaner path than taking the distribution, paying tax, and then donating the remainder."],
  [38,"Gift Tax Exclusion","/ɡɪft tæks ɪkˈskluːʒən/","noun","The annual amount that an individual can give to any other person without triggering a gift tax filing requirement or using any of the lifetime gift and estate tax exemption. The annual exclusion amount is $18,000 per recipient per year as of 2024, indexed periodically for inflation.","course","The annual exclusion is per recipient, not per donor — a married couple can give $36,000 to each recipient annually without any gift tax consequence. Consistent use of the annual exclusion over time can transfer meaningful wealth to children or other beneficiaries entirely outside the taxable estate, without touching the lifetime exemption."],
  [39,"Gift Tax Return","/ɡɪft tæks rɪˈtɜːrn/","noun","IRS Form 709, filed when a taxpayer makes gifts that exceed the annual exclusion amount to any one recipient in a calendar year, or when making certain special gifts such as contributions to 529 plans using five-year gift tax averaging. Filing the return does not necessarily mean tax is owed — it typically just records the use of lifetime exemption.","field","Most people who file a gift tax return owe no gift tax — they are simply reporting a use of their lifetime exemption. The confusion is common: filing Form 709 is not the same as paying gift tax. It is a tracking mechanism that reduces the remaining lifetime exemption by the amount reported. Tax is due only when cumulative taxable gifts exceed the lifetime exemption, which is currently very high."],
  [40,"Estate Tax Exemption","/ɪˈsteɪt tæks ɪɡˈzɛmpʃən/","noun","The amount of a deceased person's taxable estate that is exempt from federal estate tax. The exemption is currently over $13 million per individual, with a portability provision that allows a surviving spouse to use the unused portion of the deceased spouse's exemption. Estates above the exemption are taxed at a 40 percent rate on the excess.","course","The high exemption means federal estate tax affects a very small percentage of estates under current law. However, the exemption is scheduled to revert to approximately half its current level at the end of 2025 unless Congress acts — a significant planning consideration for larger estates. State estate taxes, where applicable, often have much lower exemption thresholds."],
  [41,"Generation-Skipping Tax","/ˌdʒɛnəˈreɪʃən-ˈskɪpɪŋ tæks/","noun","A tax imposed on transfers — gifts or bequests — made to individuals who are more than one generation below the transferor, such as grandchildren. It is designed to prevent wealthy families from avoiding estate tax by skipping generations. The GST tax exemption mirrors the estate tax exemption.","field","The generation-skipping tax closes a planning gap: without it, a grandparent could transfer wealth to grandchildren and skip one entire round of estate taxation that would have applied if the assets had passed through the middle generation. With the GST, the transfer is taxed at each generational level. Dynasty trusts and long-term trust structures are common planning tools for managing GST exposure."],
  [42,"Qualified Opportunity Zone","/ˈkwɒlɪfaɪd ˌɒpərˈtjuːnɪti zoʊn/","noun","A designated economically distressed community where new investments may be eligible for preferential tax treatment. Investors who reinvest capital gains into Qualified Opportunity Funds — which deploy capital in these zones — may defer, reduce, or in some cases eliminate capital gains taxes depending on the holding period and timing of the original investment.","course","Opportunity zones convert capital gains from a tax liability into a potentially tax-advantaged long-term investment. The mechanics require the reinvestment of gains within 180 days, the use of a qualified fund, and a holding period of at least 10 years to maximize the tax benefit. They appear most frequently in planning conversations following business sales, real estate dispositions, or significant investment portfolio gains. The rules are specific and the structures require professional guidance — but for the right circumstances, the tax benefit can be substantial."]
];

const savingsTerms = [
  [1,"Simple Interest","/ˈsɪmpəl ˈɪntrəst/","noun","Interest calculated only on the original principal amount, without compounding. The interest earned or owed does not itself earn interest over time. Simple interest = principal × rate × time.","field","Simple interest is the baseline — useful for understanding what compounding adds. Most savings instruments compound; most short-term loans and some personal loans use simple interest. Knowing which applies tells you whether your money is growing linearly or exponentially."],
  [2,"APY vs APR","/eɪ-piː-waɪ vɜːrsəs eɪ-piː-ɑːr/","noun","APY (Annual Percentage Yield) reflects the actual annual return on savings after compounding is applied. APR (Annual Percentage Rate) reflects the stated annual interest rate without compounding. For savings, APY is the number that matters most — it shows what you actually earn.","course","APY and APR are the same number only when interest compounds annually. The more frequently interest compounds, the larger the gap. When comparing savings accounts or CDs, always compare APY to APY — it is the only apples-to-apples measure of what you will actually accumulate."],
  [3,"Emergency Fund Sizing","/ɪˈmɜːrdʒənsi fʌnd ˈsaɪzɪŋ/","noun","The process of determining how much to hold in immediately accessible reserves to cover unexpected expenses or income disruption. Common guidance targets three to six months of essential expenses, though the right amount varies based on income stability, household structure, and risk tolerance.","field","The three-to-six month rule is a starting point, not a formula. A household with variable income, a single earner, or limited job security may need more. Someone with multiple income streams and low fixed expenses may need less. The goal is enough to navigate disruption without reaching for debt."],
  [4,"Targeted Savings","/ˈtɑːrɡɪtɪd ˈseɪvɪŋz/","noun","The practice of designating savings for a specific future goal — a home purchase, a car, a wedding, a tax bill — rather than accumulating general reserves. Each goal has its own account, timeline, and required contribution rate.","course","Targeted savings converts an abstract intention into a concrete plan. When money has a label and a deadline, the saving behavior changes. It also prevents the psychological accounting error of treating all savings as interchangeable — robbing one goal to fund another without realizing the tradeoff.","To designate a portion of savings for a specific future goal, giving the funds a named purpose, a timeline, and a required contribution rate. When a saver targets their savings, they are converting general accumulation into directed financial action.","field","To target savings is to make intention structural. The goal gets its own account, its own deadline, its own monthly number. That specificity is what separates saving that happens from saving that drifts — the label changes the behavior."],
  [5,"Automatic Transfer","/ˌɔːtəˈmætɪk ˈtrænsfɜːr/","noun","A scheduled, recurring movement of funds from one account to another — typically from checking to savings — that occurs without manual action. Automation removes the decision from the equation and ensures saving happens before spending.","field","Automatic transfers are the behavioral infrastructure of consistent saving. The research on this is clear: people save more when saving is the default, not the decision. Setting up the transfer once and leaving it in place is the simplest and most effective savings system available.","To establish a scheduled, recurring movement of funds between accounts that executes without manual intervention. A saver who automates a transfer removes willpower from the equation — the saving happens by default, before spending has a chance to absorb the funds.","course","To automate a transfer is to make saving frictionless. The decision is made once; the behavior repeats indefinitely. It is the most reliable single upgrade available to any savings system — not because the amounts are large, but because the consistency is guaranteed."],
  [6,"Round-Up Programs","/raʊnd-ʌp ˈproʊɡræmz/","noun","A savings feature offered by some banks and apps that rounds each debit card purchase up to the nearest dollar and transfers the difference to a savings account. Small, automatic, and frictionless, round-up programs accumulate savings through the residue of everyday spending.","course","Round-up programs work because they are invisible. The amounts are small enough to be unnoticed individually but meaningful in aggregate over time. They are not a primary savings strategy — but for someone struggling to start, they provide an on-ramp with essentially no friction."],
  [7,"Money Market Fund","/ˈmʌni ˈmɑːrkɪt fʌnd/","noun","A type of mutual fund that invests in short-term, high-quality debt instruments — Treasury bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit. It aims to maintain a stable $1.00 net asset value per share and pays dividends reflecting short-term interest rates. It is not FDIC insured.","field","A money market fund is not the same as a money market account at a bank. The fund is an investment product; the account is a deposit product. Both serve as cash-like holdings, but the fund carries a small risk that the bank account does not — and the fund is often held inside a brokerage rather than a bank."],
  [8,"Certificate of Deposit","/sərˈtɪfɪkət əv dɪˈpɒzɪt/","noun","A time deposit offered by banks and credit unions that pays a fixed interest rate in exchange for leaving a set amount of money untouched for a specified term — typically ranging from one month to five years. Early withdrawal typically incurs a penalty.","course","A CD exchanges liquidity for yield. The rate is locked at purchase, which is an advantage when rates are falling and a disadvantage when they rise. The penalty for early withdrawal is the cost of reclaiming flexibility — worth knowing before committing funds to a term that may no longer fit."],
  [9,"CD Ladder","/siː-diː ˈlædər/","noun","A savings strategy that divides funds across multiple CDs with staggered maturity dates — for example, one, two, three, four, and five year terms. As each CD matures, the proceeds are reinvested in a new long-term CD, creating regular access to funds while capturing longer-term rates.","field","A CD ladder solves the core tension of CD investing: locking in higher rates without locking up all funds for the same period. As each rung matures, the saver has a decision point — reinvest, redirect, or access — without penalty. It is a disciplined, low-complexity approach to fixed-rate savings.","To build a series of CDs with staggered maturity dates, distributing funds across multiple terms so that a portion becomes available at regular intervals while the rest continues earning longer-term rates. Laddering a CD position creates both yield and liquidity.","field","To ladder CDs is to engineer flexibility into a fixed-rate instrument. Instead of committing everything to one term, the saver staggers the maturities — one year, two years, three years — so each rung arrives on schedule. The result is regular access without sacrificing the rate premium that longer terms provide."],
  [10,"Brokered CD","/ˈbroʊkərd siː-diː/","noun","A certificate of deposit purchased through a brokerage firm rather than directly from a bank. Brokered CDs can be bought and sold on the secondary market before maturity, offering more liquidity than bank CDs — but at a market price that may be above or below face value.","course","Brokered CDs appear in brokerage accounts alongside other fixed income instruments. They are FDIC insured up to applicable limits, but if sold before maturity, the price depends on prevailing interest rates — selling into a rising rate environment may produce a loss. Understand the difference between holding to maturity and selling early before using them."],
  [11,"FDIC Insurance Limits","/ɛf-diː-aɪ-siː ɪnˈʃʊərəns ˈlɪmɪts/","noun","The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation insures deposits at member banks up to $250,000 per depositor, per institution, per ownership category. Ownership categories — individual, joint, retirement, trust — each carry their own $250,000 limit, allowing a single depositor to hold significantly more than $250,000 in insured deposits at one institution through proper structuring.","field","The $250,000 limit is per ownership category, not per account. A married couple with joint and individual accounts at the same bank may have significantly more than $250,000 in insured coverage. For those holding large cash balances, understanding the categories — and potentially spreading funds across institutions — is a meaningful protection consideration."],
  [12,"NCUA","/ɛn-siː-juː-eɪ/","noun","The National Credit Union Administration — the federal agency that insures deposits at federally insured credit unions, providing coverage equivalent to FDIC insurance at banks: $250,000 per depositor, per institution, per ownership category.","course","NCUA coverage works identically to FDIC coverage for practical purposes. Deposits at credit unions are as federally protected as deposits at banks. The distinction matters only when deciding whether to use a bank or credit union — the insurance protection should not be a differentiating factor."],
  [13,"Treasury Bills","/ˈtrɛʒəri bɪlz/","noun","Short-term debt obligations issued by the U.S. federal government with maturities of four weeks to one year. T-bills are sold at a discount to face value and pay no periodic interest — the return is the difference between the purchase price and the face value received at maturity.","field","Treasury bills are the closest thing to risk-free in the financial system — backed by the full faith and credit of the U.S. government. They are a benchmark for short-term rates and a practical alternative to savings accounts when rates are favorable, particularly for larger cash holdings."],
  [14,"Treasury Notes","/ˈtrɛʒəri noʊts/","noun","Medium-term debt obligations issued by the U.S. government with maturities of two to ten years. Unlike T-bills, Treasury notes pay semi-annual interest at a fixed coupon rate, plus the return of face value at maturity.","course","Treasury notes occupy the middle of the yield curve — longer than bills, shorter than bonds. Their semi-annual interest payments and government backing make them a reliable fixed income option for intermediate savings goals and a standard component of conservative portfolios."],
  [15,"Treasury Bonds","/ˈtrɛʒəri bɒndz/","noun","Long-term debt obligations issued by the U.S. government with maturities of 20 or 30 years. Like Treasury notes, they pay semi-annual interest at a fixed rate. They are sensitive to interest rate changes — prices move inversely to rates, with longer maturities experiencing greater price swings.","field","Treasury bonds provide the longest-duration government income available. Their price sensitivity to interest rate changes is significant at 20 to 30 year maturities — a consideration when interest rate risk matters as much as credit risk. They are income instruments, not savings instruments, for most holding periods."],
  [16,"TIPS","/tɪps/","noun","Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities — U.S. government bonds whose principal value adjusts with inflation as measured by the Consumer Price Index. Interest is paid semi-annually on the inflation-adjusted principal. At maturity, the holder receives the greater of the adjusted principal or original face value.","course","TIPS are the government's built-in hedge against inflation eroding fixed income returns. When inflation rises, the principal grows — and interest payments grow with it. They are particularly relevant for long-term savings goals where purchasing power preservation is as important as nominal return."],
  [17,"I-Bonds","/aɪ-bɒndz/","noun","Series I savings bonds issued by the U.S. Treasury that earn interest based on a combination of a fixed rate and an inflation adjustment that changes semi-annually. They protect purchasing power directly and are exempt from state and local taxes. Annual purchase limits apply.","field","I-bonds have a purchase ceiling — $10,000 per person per year through TreasuryDirect, with an additional $5,000 available through tax refunds. Within that limit, they offer one of the most direct inflation protections available to individual savers. The one-year lockup and early redemption penalty in the first five years are the liquidity tradeoffs."],
  [18,"Savings Bonds","/ˈseɪvɪŋz bɒndz/","noun","U.S. government debt instruments sold directly to individual investors, not through secondary markets. Series EE bonds grow at a fixed rate and are guaranteed to double in value if held 20 years. Series I bonds adjust for inflation. Both are exempt from state and local taxes and can be tax-free when used for qualified education expenses.","course","Savings bonds are a distinctive instrument: they cannot be transferred or sold, only redeemed. That illiquidity is part of their design — they are savings vehicles, not investment vehicles. The education tax exclusion makes them a useful tool for long-term education savings when the income requirements are met."],
  [19,"Joint Account Titling","/dʒɔɪnt əˈkaʊnt ˈtaɪtəlɪŋ/","noun","The way in which a jointly held account is legally structured, determining rights of survivorship and creditor access. The two most common forms are Joint Tenants with Rights of Survivorship (JTWROS), where the surviving owner inherits automatically, and Tenants in Common (TIC), where each owner's share passes through their estate.","field","Titling is not a detail — it determines who owns what and what happens when one owner dies. JTWROS passes outside probate directly to the survivor. TIC passes through the estate according to the will. Choosing the wrong form can produce outcomes the account holders never intended."],
  [20,"TOD Designation","/tiː-oʊ-diː ˌdɛzɪɡˈneɪʃən/","noun","Transfer on Death — a beneficiary designation on a financial account that directs the account balance to a named individual or entity upon the account owner's death, bypassing probate. TOD designations can be changed at any time and take precedence over instructions in a will.","course","A TOD designation is one of the simplest and most effective estate planning tools available. It costs nothing to establish, requires no attorney, and ensures the account passes directly and immediately to the intended recipient. Keeping TOD designations current — especially after life changes — is essential maintenance.","To name a beneficiary on a financial account who will receive its assets directly upon the owner's death, bypassing probate. When an account holder designates a TOD beneficiary, they are establishing a transfer mechanism that operates outside the will and takes immediate effect at death.","field","To designate a TOD beneficiary is to settle a question in advance — simply, at no cost, with no attorney required. The account passes directly to the named person. The designation must be kept current; a name that was right at 40 may not be right at 65. Review it whenever life changes."],
  [21,"UGMA","/ˈʌɡmə/","noun","Uniform Gifts to Minors Act — a custodial account that allows an adult to hold and manage assets on behalf of a minor. The assets are irrevocably transferred to the minor at a legally defined age, typically 18 or 21, at which point the minor gains full control. Contributions are not tax-deductible and investment gains are subject to the Kiddie Tax rules for minors.","field","UGMA accounts are simple and flexible — no restrictions on how funds are used once the minor reaches the transfer age. That flexibility is also the limitation: there is no mechanism to delay or condition the transfer. The full account becomes the minor's asset unconditionally at the designated age."],
  [22,"UTMA","/ˈʌtmə/","noun","Uniform Transfers to Minors Act — similar to a UGMA account but broader in the types of assets that can be held, including real estate and other property in addition to financial assets. Transfer ages may extend to 25 in some states, providing a slightly longer custodial window than UGMA.","course","UTMA is the more widely adopted and flexible successor to UGMA. The practical difference for most families is modest — both hold financial assets for minors, both transfer control at majority, both carry the same tax treatment. The broader asset class eligibility and potentially extended transfer age are the distinguishing features."],
  [23,"Coverdell ESA","/ˈkʌvərdɛl iː-ɛs-eɪ/","noun","A Coverdell Education Savings Account — a tax-advantaged account that allows after-tax contributions of up to $2,000 per year per beneficiary for qualified education expenses at elementary, secondary, or higher education levels. Earnings grow tax-free; withdrawals for qualified expenses are tax-free. Contribution eligibility phases out at higher income levels.","field","The Coverdell's contribution limit is low relative to education costs, but its coverage of K-12 expenses — which a 529 plan did not originally include — made it distinctive. For families with education savings strategies that span primary school through college, it can complement a 529. Income limits restrict high earners from contributing directly."],
  [24,"HSA as Savings Vehicle","/eɪtʃ-ɛs-eɪ æz ˈseɪvɪŋz ˈviːɪkəl/","noun","The use of a Health Savings Account as a long-term investment and savings tool beyond its primary function of paying medical expenses. Contributions are pre-tax, growth is tax-free, and withdrawals for qualified medical expenses are tax-free — a triple tax advantage. After age 65, withdrawals for any purpose are taxed as ordinary income, making the HSA function like a traditional IRA for non-medical expenses.","course","The HSA is the most tax-advantaged account in the system when used strategically. Contributing the maximum, investing the balance, paying current medical expenses out of pocket, and preserving the account for future healthcare costs — or retirement — extracts the full triple-tax benefit. It requires the discipline not to spend what is available."],
  [25,"Defined Benefit vs Defined Contribution","/dɪˈfaɪnd ˈbɛnɪfɪt vɜːrsəs dɪˈfaɪnd ˌkɒntrɪˈbjuːʃən/","noun","Two fundamental structures for employer-sponsored retirement plans. A defined benefit plan — the traditional pension — promises a specified monthly benefit at retirement based on salary and years of service. A defined contribution plan — such as a 401(k) — specifies how much is contributed but makes no guarantee about the eventual benefit, which depends on investment returns.","field","The shift from defined benefit to defined contribution over the past four decades transferred investment risk from employer to employee. Pensions guarantee an outcome; 401(k)s guarantee an input. The difference changes everything about retirement planning — who bears market risk, who makes investment decisions, and what security looks like in retirement."],
  [26,"Solo 401(k)","/ˈsoʊloʊ fɔːr-oʊ-wʌn-keɪ/","noun","A 401(k) plan designed for self-employed individuals with no employees other than a spouse. It allows contributions in two capacities — as both employer and employee — enabling significantly higher annual contributions than a SEP-IRA or SIMPLE IRA in many income scenarios. Roth contribution options are available in some plans.","course","The solo 401(k) is the most powerful retirement savings vehicle available to the self-employed at most income levels. The dual contribution capacity — up to the employee deferral limit plus an employer profit-sharing contribution — can shelter a substantial portion of self-employment income. Plan administration requirements are modest when there are no employees."],
  [27,"SEP-IRA","/sɛp-aɪ-ɑːr-eɪ/","noun","A Simplified Employee Pension IRA — a retirement plan for self-employed individuals and small business owners that allows employer contributions of up to 25 percent of compensation or a defined annual maximum, whichever is less. Contributions are tax-deductible; growth is tax-deferred; withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income.","field","The SEP-IRA is easy to establish and administer — no annual filings, no complex plan documents. For sole proprietors and small business owners looking for a straightforward high-contribution vehicle, it is often the first choice. The limitation relative to a solo 401(k): no Roth option and no employee salary deferral component."],
  [28,"SIMPLE IRA","/ˈsɪmpəl aɪ-ɑːr-eɪ/","noun","A Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees — a retirement plan for small businesses with 100 or fewer employees. Employees can make salary deferrals up to an annual limit, and employers are required to make either matching contributions or non-elective contributions. Contribution limits are lower than 401(k) limits.","course","The SIMPLE IRA sits between the SEP-IRA and a full 401(k) in complexity and contribution capacity. It requires employer contributions — a mandatory cost — but provides employees with a salary deferral mechanism. For small business owners who want to offer a retirement benefit with minimal administrative burden, it is a practical starting point."],
  [29,"Contribution Limits","/ˌkɒntrɪˈbjuːʃən ˈlɪmɪts/","noun","The maximum amounts that can be contributed annually to tax-advantaged retirement accounts, as set by the IRS and adjusted periodically for inflation. Limits vary by account type — 401(k), IRA, SEP-IRA — and may differ based on age and employment status.","field","Contribution limits are the ceiling on tax-advantaged savings — and the first number to know when building a retirement savings strategy. Hitting the limit on every available account each year is one of the most consistent levers available for building long-term wealth with the greatest tax efficiency."],
  [30,"Catch-Up Contributions","/kætʃ-ʌp ˌkɒntrɪˈbjuːʃənz/","noun","Additional contributions permitted to tax-advantaged retirement accounts for individuals age 50 and older, above the standard annual limits. Available in 401(k), IRA, and most employer-sponsored plans. The catch-up provision acknowledges that many people increase savings capacity in their later working years.","course","Catch-up contributions are a meaningful accelerant in the final years before retirement. The additional capacity — several thousand dollars per year depending on the account — compounds forward even over a shorter horizon. For those who started saving late or interrupted savings during career transitions, they provide a structural opportunity to close the gap."],
  [31,"After-Tax Contributions","/ˈɑːftər-tæks ˌkɒntrɪˈbjuːʃənz/","noun","Contributions made to a retirement account using dollars that have already been taxed, distinct from pre-tax contributions that reduce current taxable income. After-tax contributions to a 401(k) are not the same as Roth contributions — they go into a separate bucket and are tracked differently, but they form the foundation for the mega backdoor Roth strategy.","field","After-tax 401(k) contributions matter primarily as the raw material for the mega backdoor Roth conversion. The contributions themselves go in without a tax deduction — but converting them to Roth status allows future growth to be tax-free. Without the conversion, after-tax contributions offer limited advantage over a taxable account."],
  [32,"Backdoor Roth","/ˈbækdɔːr roʊθ/","noun","A strategy that allows individuals whose income exceeds the Roth IRA contribution limits to effectively contribute to a Roth IRA by making a non-deductible traditional IRA contribution and then converting it to a Roth IRA. The conversion is taxable only on any earnings that accrued between contribution and conversion.","course","The backdoor Roth is a legal workaround for high earners locked out of direct Roth contributions. It requires attention to the pro-rata rule — if the contributor holds other pre-tax IRA funds, a portion of the conversion may be taxable. Converting promptly after contributing minimizes taxable earnings on the converted amount.","To execute the backdoor Roth strategy: make a non-deductible traditional IRA contribution and promptly convert it to a Roth IRA, effectively funding a Roth account despite exceeding the direct income contribution limits. The mechanics are straightforward; the pro-rata rule is the variable that requires attention.","field","To backdoor Roth is to use the available legal path when the direct path is closed. The income limit applies to direct Roth contributions — not to conversions. The contribution goes in as non-deductible; the conversion moves it to Roth. Done quickly, the taxable amount is negligible. Done without checking existing IRA balances, the pro-rata rule can produce an unpleasant surprise."],
  [33,"Mega Backdoor Roth","/ˈmɛɡə ˈbækdɔːr roʊθ/","noun","An advanced strategy available in 401(k) plans that permit after-tax contributions and in-service withdrawals or conversions. By contributing after-tax dollars beyond the standard 401(k) limit and immediately converting or rolling them to a Roth account, high earners can accumulate substantially more in tax-free retirement savings than standard limits allow.","field","The mega backdoor Roth can meaningfully increase the total amount saved in tax-free accounts — but it requires a 401(k) plan that explicitly allows after-tax contributions and in-service distributions or conversions. Not all plans do. Confirming plan eligibility is the first step before incorporating the strategy."],
  [34,"Rollover vs Transfer","/ˈroʊloʊvər vɜːrsəs ˈtrænsfɜːr/","noun","Two methods for moving retirement funds between accounts. A rollover involves the account holder receiving the funds and redepositing them into a new account within 60 days — one rollover per 12-month period is permitted. A direct transfer moves funds institution-to-institution without the account holder ever receiving them, with no frequency limits and no withholding.","course","The distinction matters because a rollover handled incorrectly becomes a taxable distribution. If the 60-day window is missed or the one-per-year limit is exceeded, the funds are treated as income. Direct transfers avoid both risks entirely. When moving retirement funds, a direct trustee-to-trustee transfer is nearly always the cleaner path.","To move retirement funds from one account to another by receiving the distribution personally and redepositing it within 60 days. Rolling over funds differs from a direct transfer in that the account holder takes temporary possession of the money, triggering withholding requirements and a hard deadline.","course","To roll over retirement funds is to take responsibility for the move. The funds land in your hands — or a check is made out to you — and the 60-day clock starts. Miss the deadline or exceed the one-per-year limit and the distribution becomes taxable income. The direct transfer is safer; the rollover is riskier and should be used only when the direct path isn't available."],
  [35,"Direct Rollover","/dɪˈrɛkt ˈroʊloʊvər/","noun","A retirement fund transfer in which funds move directly from one employer-sponsored plan to another qualified plan or IRA, without passing through the account holder's hands. No mandatory withholding applies, and no 60-day deadline exists because the account holder never receives the funds.","field","A direct rollover is the safest mechanism for moving an employer plan balance when changing jobs or retiring. The funds never leave the tax-advantaged system, no withholding is triggered, and there is no deadline pressure. Requesting a direct rollover rather than a check made out to the account holder prevents the most common rollover mistakes."],
  [36,"72(t) Distributions","/ˈsɛvənti-tuː tiː dɪˌstrɪˈbjuːʃənz/","noun","A provision of the IRS tax code that allows penalty-free withdrawals from an IRA or 401(k) before age 59½, provided the account holder takes a series of Substantially Equal Periodic Payments (SEPP) based on life expectancy. The payments must continue for at least five years or until age 59½, whichever is longer.","course","72(t) distributions are a specific and rigid tool for accessing retirement funds early without the 10 percent penalty. The calculation method is fixed, the payment schedule cannot be changed without triggering penalties retroactively, and the commitment is multi-year. It is a solution for a specific circumstance — not a casual early withdrawal mechanism."],
  [37,"Required Beginning Date","/rɪˈkwaɪərd bɪˈɡɪnɪŋ deɪt/","noun","The date by which an account holder must begin taking Required Minimum Distributions from traditional IRAs and most employer-sponsored retirement plans. Currently April 1 of the year following the year in which the account holder reaches age 73 under current law, though the date has been adjusted several times by legislation.","field","The required beginning date is not optional — missing it triggers a significant penalty. Taking the first distribution by April 1 means two distributions in the first year, which can push income into a higher bracket. For most people, taking the first RMD in the year they turn 73 rather than waiting until April 1 of the following year is the cleaner approach."],
  [38,"Stretch IRA","/strɛtʃ aɪ-ɑːr-eɪ/","noun","A former estate planning strategy that allowed non-spouse IRA beneficiaries to take distributions over their own life expectancy, stretching the tax-deferred growth across decades. The SECURE Act of 2019 largely eliminated the stretch IRA for most non-spouse beneficiaries, replacing it with a 10-year distribution rule.","course","The stretch IRA is largely gone for most beneficiaries — but the term still circulates, and understanding what it was clarifies what changed. Beneficiaries who inherited before the SECURE Act may still operate under the old rules. For most heirs today, the 10-year rule applies: the entire account must be distributed within 10 years of the original owner's death."],
  [39,"Inherited IRA Rules","/ɪnˈhɛrɪtɪd aɪ-ɑːr-eɪ ruːlz/","noun","The regulations governing how a beneficiary must take distributions from an IRA they did not fund themselves. Rules vary based on the relationship to the original owner and when the account was inherited. Surviving spouses have the most flexibility; most other beneficiaries are subject to the 10-year rule under the SECURE Act.","field","Inherited IRA rules changed significantly with the SECURE Act and again with SECURE 2.0. Surviving spouses can roll the account into their own IRA or treat it as an inherited IRA. Most non-spouse beneficiaries must empty the account within 10 years. The specific rules — including whether annual distributions are required within the 10-year window — depend on whether the original owner had begun RMDs."],
  [40,"Pension Lump Sum vs Annuity Option","/ˈpɛnʃən lʌmp sʌm vɜːrsəs əˈnjuːɪti ˈɒpʃən/","noun","A choice offered by some defined benefit pension plans at retirement: receive the full present value of the pension as a single lump-sum payment, or receive guaranteed monthly income for life — or a specified period — as an annuity. The decision is typically irrevocable once made.","course","The lump sum vs annuity decision is one of the most consequential and irreversible financial choices many people will make. The lump sum offers control, flexibility, and the ability to leave assets to heirs — but requires disciplined investment and carries longevity risk. The annuity offers guaranteed income that cannot be outlived — but surrenders control and typically leaves nothing to heirs. The right answer depends on health, other income sources, risk tolerance, and whether leaving a legacy matters."]
];

const protectionTerms = [
  [1,"Insurable Interest","/ɪnˈʃʊərəbəl ˈɪntrəst/","noun","A legitimate financial stake in the continued life, health, or property of the person or thing being insured. Insurable interest must exist at the time a policy is issued — it is the legal and ethical foundation that distinguishes insurance from a wager.","field","You cannot insure what you have no stake in losing. Insurable interest is the requirement that keeps insurance grounded in genuine protection rather than speculation. It applies to life, health, and property policies alike."],
  [2,"Underwriting","/ˈʌndərˌraɪtɪŋ/","noun","The process by which an insurer evaluates the risk of insuring a person or property, determines eligibility, and sets the premium accordingly. Underwriters assess factors such as age, health history, occupation, and lifestyle to price the policy.","course","Underwriting is the insurer's risk assessment. The result — approval, modified terms, or denial — reflects the insurer's calculation of likelihood and cost. Understanding what underwriters evaluate helps explain why similar people may receive different offers.","To evaluate the risk associated with insuring a person or property and determine the terms, premium, or eligibility of coverage. When an insurer underwrites an application, it is measuring how much risk it is agreeing to absorb.","field","To be underwritten is to be assessed — not judged. The underwriting process prices risk, and everything disclosed in an application shapes that pricing. Accurate and complete disclosure at this stage is both an ethical requirement and a practical protection against later claim denial."],
  [3,"Guaranteed Issue","/ˌɡærənˈtiːd ˈɪʃuː/","noun","A policy that is issued without medical underwriting — the applicant cannot be denied coverage based on health status. Guaranteed issue policies are available regardless of pre-existing conditions, but typically carry higher premiums and lower death benefits.","field","Guaranteed issue removes the health barrier to obtaining coverage. The tradeoff is cost and limits. It exists precisely for those who cannot qualify through standard underwriting — a meaningful option for a specific circumstance, not a default choice."],
  [4,"Graded Death Benefit","/ˈɡreɪdɪd dɛθ ˈbɛnɪfɪt/","noun","A life insurance feature common in guaranteed issue policies that limits the death benefit during an initial period — typically two to three years. If the insured dies during this window from natural causes, beneficiaries receive a return of premiums plus interest rather than the full face amount.","course","A graded death benefit is the insurer's hedge against adverse selection in guaranteed issue products. The full benefit is eventually available — but the waiting period is a material limitation that must be understood before relying on the policy for immediate coverage needs."],
  [5,"Rider","/ˈraɪdər/","noun","An optional provision added to an insurance policy that expands, restricts, or modifies the base coverage. Riders allow policyholders to customize a policy to their specific needs — for an additional premium in most cases.","field","Riders are where a policy becomes personal. The base policy covers the standard case; riders address your specific circumstances. Understanding which riders are available — and which ones are relevant — is as important as understanding the policy itself.","To add an optional provision to an existing insurance policy, modifying or expanding its base coverage for an additional premium. A policyholder who riders their policy is customizing it to fit circumstances the base contract does not address.","course","To rider a policy is to make it yours. The base coverage addresses the general case; the rider addresses your specific situation — a disability waiver, an accelerated benefit, a return of premium feature. Each addition has a cost; the question is whether the protection it adds is worth it."],
  [6,"Term Life","/tɜːrm laɪf/","noun","Life insurance that provides a death benefit for a specified period — typically 10, 20, or 30 years. If the insured dies during the term, the death benefit is paid. If the term expires without a claim, the coverage ends with no residual value.","course","Term life is the most direct form of life insurance: pure protection for a defined window of time. It is the most affordable way to secure a significant death benefit and is well suited to covering obligations with a known endpoint — a mortgage, a child's dependency, income replacement during working years."],
  [7,"Return of Premium","/rɪˈtɜːrn əv ˈpriːmiəm/","noun","A term life insurance rider or policy feature that refunds all or a portion of premiums paid if the insured outlives the policy term. It converts a 'use it or lose it' policy into one with a guaranteed return if no claim is made.","field","Return of premium costs more than standard term — sometimes significantly. Whether that additional cost is worth the return of unused premiums is a straightforward calculation, but one that depends entirely on the individual's goals and alternatives for those dollars over the same period."],
  [8,"Whole Life","/hoʊl laɪf/","noun","Permanent life insurance that provides lifelong coverage with a fixed premium, a guaranteed death benefit, and a cash value component that grows at a guaranteed rate over time. The policy does not expire as long as premiums are paid.","course","Whole life is a contract of permanence. The certainty it offers — fixed premiums, guaranteed growth, a death benefit that will be paid — comes at a substantially higher cost than term. It is a fit for specific planning objectives, not a universal solution."],
  [9,"Cash Value","/kæʃ ˈvæljuː/","noun","The savings or investment component of a permanent life insurance policy that accumulates over time. Cash value grows tax-deferred, can be accessed through loans or withdrawals, and is distinct from the death benefit — though accessing it can affect the benefit paid at death.","field","Cash value is the living benefit of a permanent policy — available to the policyholder during their lifetime. Its growth, accessibility, and interaction with the death benefit vary by policy type. Understanding the mechanics before accessing it prevents unintended consequences."],
  [10,"Policy Loans","/ˈpɒlɪsi loʊnz/","noun","Loans taken against the cash value of a permanent life insurance policy. The insurer lends the policyholder a portion of the accumulated cash value, using it as collateral. Interest accrues on the loan, and unpaid balances reduce the death benefit.","course","Policy loans are not withdrawals — the cash value remains intact and continues to grow. But the loan balance, including accruing interest, must be managed. If it grows unchecked and exceeds the cash value, the policy can lapse, potentially creating a taxable event and eliminating coverage."],
  [11,"Surrender","/səˈrɛndər/","noun","The voluntary termination of a permanent life insurance or annuity policy by the policyholder in exchange for its cash surrender value — the accumulated cash value minus any applicable surrender charges. Surrendering ends coverage permanently.","field","Surrender charges are the insurer's protection against early exit. They are most significant in the early years and most commonly encountered when circumstances change and the policy no longer fits. Knowing the surrender schedule before purchasing is essential — it defines when flexibility becomes available.","To voluntarily terminate a permanent life insurance or annuity policy and receive its cash surrender value in return. A policyholder who surrenders a policy is ending the coverage contract and accepting whatever net cash value remains after charges.","course","To surrender a policy is to close a chapter — permanently. The cash received may feel like a gain, but the protection ends with it, and any gain above basis may be taxable. Before surrendering, understand the surrender charge schedule, the tax implications, and whether a 1035 exchange or loan might better serve the underlying need."],
  [12,"Universal Life","/ˌjuːnɪˈvɜːrsəl laɪf/","noun","A form of permanent life insurance that offers flexible premiums and an adjustable death benefit, with a cash value component that earns interest based on current market rates or a declared minimum. Unlike whole life, the policyholder can vary payment amounts within limits.","course","Universal life trades whole life's rigidity for flexibility — but flexibility requires active management. Underpaying premiums over time can erode cash value and eventually cause the policy to lapse. The flexibility is real; so is the responsibility to monitor it."],
  [13,"Variable Life","/ˈvɛəriəbəl laɪf/","noun","A permanent life insurance policy in which the cash value is invested in sub-accounts — similar to mutual funds — whose performance directly affects the cash value and, in some versions, the death benefit. Returns are not guaranteed and can decline.","field","Variable life introduces market risk into life insurance. The potential for higher cash value growth comes with the possibility of loss. The death benefit may have a guaranteed floor, but the cash value does not. It is a product that requires investment sophistication alongside insurance planning."],
  [14,"Indexed Universal Life","/ˈɪndɛkst ˌjuːnɪˈvɜːrsəl laɪf/","noun","A form of universal life insurance in which cash value growth is linked to the performance of a market index — such as the S&P 500 — subject to a cap on gains and a floor that prevents losses. Premiums are flexible, and the death benefit is adjustable.","course","Indexed universal life offers a middle position between fixed universal life and variable life: market-linked upside with downside protection. The cap limits how much of the index's gain is credited, and the floor prevents negative crediting. Understanding both boundaries is essential to realistic expectations."],
  [15,"Death Benefit","/dɛθ ˈbɛnɪfɪt/","noun","The amount paid to the beneficiary upon the insured's death. In term and whole life policies, it is typically a fixed amount. In variable and indexed policies, it may fluctuate based on investment performance or cash value. Outstanding policy loans reduce the net amount received.","field","The death benefit is the reason the policy exists. Everything else — premiums, cash value, riders — surrounds and supports this core promise. Knowing exactly what the beneficiary will receive, under what conditions, and net of any outstanding loans or charges, is the foundation of any protection plan."],
  [16,"Waiver of Premium","/ˈweɪvər əv ˈpriːmiəm/","noun","A rider that suspends premium payments if the policyholder becomes totally disabled and unable to work, while keeping the policy in force. Coverage continues during the disability period without requiring payment.","course","Waiver of premium protects the insurance itself during a disability — ensuring that the loss of income doesn't also mean the loss of coverage. It is particularly valuable on permanent policies with significant cash value, where lapsing the policy would have long-term consequences."],
  [17,"Accidental Death Benefit","/əkˈsɪdəntəl dɛθ ˈbɛnɪfɪt/","noun","A rider that pays an additional death benefit — often equal to the base face amount — if the insured dies as a result of an accident rather than illness or natural causes. Sometimes called double indemnity.","field","Accidental death benefit is supplemental coverage for a specific cause of death. It adds a layer on top of the base policy for accidental circumstances. Whether the additional premium is justified depends on the overall coverage picture — it is not a substitute for adequate base coverage."],
  [18,"Contestability Period","/kənˌtɛstəˈbɪlɪti ˈpɪəriəd/","noun","The first two years of a life insurance policy during which the insurer has the right to investigate and potentially deny a claim if it discovers material misrepresentation or fraud in the application. After this period, the policy becomes incontestable on most grounds.","course","The contestability period is the insurer's window to verify what was represented at application. Accurate disclosure matters most here — omissions or misstatements that might otherwise go unnoticed can become grounds for denial if a claim occurs during this period."],
  [19,"Incontestability Clause","/ˌɪnkənˌtɛstəˈbɪlɪti klɔːz/","noun","A provision in a life insurance policy stating that after a specified period — typically two years — the insurer cannot contest the validity of the policy on the basis of misrepresentation in the application, except in cases of fraud.","field","The incontestability clause is the policyholder's long-term protection against retroactive denial. Once the period passes, the policy's validity is largely settled. It represents a fundamental fairness principle: the insurer had its window to investigate, and that window has closed."],
  [20,"Per Stirpes","/pɜːr ˈstɜːrpiːz/","noun","A Latin legal term meaning 'by the branch,' used in beneficiary designations and estate planning. Under a per stirpes distribution, if a primary beneficiary predeceases the insured, that beneficiary's share passes to their descendants rather than being redistributed among the surviving primary beneficiaries.","course","Per stirpes is the default in many jurisdictions and the designation that most accurately preserves generational intent. Without it, a deceased beneficiary's share may pass to unintended parties. It is a small phrase with large consequences — worth specifying deliberately rather than discovering accidentally."],
  [21,"Irrevocable Beneficiary","/ɪˈrɛvəkəbəl ˌbɛnɪˈfɪʃəri/","noun","A beneficiary designation that cannot be changed without the beneficiary's consent. Unlike a revocable beneficiary, an irrevocable beneficiary has vested rights in the policy — the policyholder cannot reassign, surrender, or take loans against the policy without that beneficiary's approval.","field","Irrevocable beneficiary designations are most common in divorce settlements and business agreements. They provide the beneficiary with legally enforceable rights — which also means the policyholder surrenders unilateral control. The designation should be made intentionally and with full understanding of what it limits."],
  [22,"Short-Term Disability","/ʃɔːrt-tɜːrm ˌdɪsəˈbɪlɪti/","noun","Insurance that replaces a portion of income — typically 60 to 70 percent — during a temporary disability that prevents the insured from working, usually covering periods from a few weeks up to six months. Many employer-sponsored benefit packages include short-term disability coverage.","course","Short-term disability bridges the gap between the onset of an illness or injury and either recovery or the activation of long-term disability coverage. The elimination period — the waiting time before benefits begin — is typically short, often just a week or two."],
  [23,"Long-Term Disability","/lɒŋ-tɜːrm ˌdɪsəˈbɪlɪti/","noun","Insurance that replaces a portion of income when a disability prevents the insured from working for an extended period — typically beyond three to six months — and may continue for years or until retirement age. It is the most financially consequential protection most working people are underinsured against.","field","The probability of a disabling event during a working career is higher than most people recognize — and the financial impact of lost income for years is far greater than most early deaths. Long-term disability insurance is, for most people in their working years, as important as life insurance."],
  [24,"Own-Occupation Definition","/oʊn-ˌɒkjʊˈpeɪʃən ˌdɛfɪˈnɪʃən/","noun","A disability insurance definition under which a claimant is considered disabled — and eligible for benefits — if they are unable to perform the specific duties of their own occupation, even if they are capable of working in a different field. The most comprehensive and favorable definition for the policyholder.","course","Own-occupation coverage preserves the most: a surgeon who can no longer perform surgery collects benefits even while working in another capacity. The definition matters enormously when a claim is filed — it determines whether a partial or career-ending disability triggers the benefit."],
  [25,"Any-Occupation Definition","/ˈɛni-ˌɒkjʊˈpeɪʃən ˌdɛfɪˈnɪʃən/","noun","A disability insurance definition under which a claimant is considered disabled only if they are unable to perform any gainful occupation for which they are reasonably suited by education, training, or experience. A more restrictive standard that is harder to meet than own-occupation.","field","Any-occupation policies are less expensive — because they pay out less frequently. The claimant must be unable to work in virtually any reasonable capacity to qualify. For professionals with specialized skills, the distinction between own-occupation and any-occupation coverage can represent the difference between receiving benefits and not."],
  [26,"Elimination Period","/ɪˌlɪmɪˈneɪʃən ˈpɪəriəd/","noun","The waiting period at the beginning of a disability claim during which benefits are not yet paid. The insured must be continuously disabled for the length of the elimination period before benefits begin. Common periods are 30, 60, 90, or 180 days.","course","The elimination period functions like a deductible measured in time. A longer elimination period means lower premiums — but requires the claimant to have sufficient reserves to cover the waiting window. Matching the elimination period to available emergency reserves is a key design decision."],
  [27,"Benefit Period","/ˈbɛnɪfɪt ˈpɪəriəd/","noun","The maximum length of time disability insurance benefits will be paid during a qualifying disability. Common periods range from two years to five years to age 65 or 67. Longer benefit periods provide greater protection and carry higher premiums.","field","The benefit period defines the coverage horizon. A two-year benefit period may address most disabilities but leaves a gap if the condition is permanent. For income replacement planning, a benefit period extending to retirement age provides the most complete protection against a long-term or permanent disability."],
  [28,"HRA","/eɪtʃ-ɑːr-eɪ/","noun","A Health Reimbursement Arrangement — an employer-funded account used to reimburse employees for qualified medical expenses and, in some cases, health insurance premiums. Unlike an HSA, an HRA is owned and funded entirely by the employer; the employee contributes nothing.","course","An HRA is an employer benefit, not a personal account. The funds belong to the employer until used — they typically do not roll over if the employee leaves. Understanding what expenses qualify and how the funds interact with other health coverage helps employees use the benefit fully."],
  [29,"Copay","/ˈkoʊpeɪ/","noun","A fixed dollar amount paid by the insured for a specific healthcare service — such as a doctor's visit or prescription — at the time of service. The insurer pays the remainder. Copays do not typically count toward the deductible but may count toward the out-of-pocket maximum.","field","Copays are the most visible part of health insurance cost — paid every time a service is used. They provide predictability for routine care. Knowing which services carry copays versus coinsurance versus full cost-sharing helps avoid unexpected bills."],
  [30,"Coinsurance","/ˌkoʊɪnˈʃʊərəns/","noun","The percentage of covered medical costs the insured pays after the deductible has been met. For example, an 80/20 coinsurance arrangement means the insurer pays 80 percent and the insured pays 20 percent of covered costs until the out-of-pocket maximum is reached.","course","Coinsurance is the shared responsibility layer between deductible and out-of-pocket maximum. It applies to larger or more complex claims — the ones where the actual dollar exposure becomes significant. Understanding the percentage and how quickly it might accumulate is essential for planning around health care costs."],
  [31,"Network","/ˈnɛtwɜːrk/","noun","The group of healthcare providers — physicians, hospitals, specialists, labs — that have contracted with an insurance company to provide services at negotiated rates. Services received in-network are covered at the plan's standard rate; out-of-network care typically costs significantly more or may not be covered at all.","field","Network is one of the most practically important features of a health plan, and one of the most overlooked during enrollment. Before choosing a plan, confirming that preferred providers, hospitals, and specialists are in-network prevents significant unexpected cost."],
  [32,"Formulary","/ˈfɔːrmjʊləri/","noun","A list of prescription drugs covered by a health insurance plan, organized into tiers that determine the cost-sharing for each medication. Drugs on the formulary are covered at defined rates; drugs not on the formulary may not be covered or may require prior authorization.","course","The formulary determines what your prescriptions will actually cost under a specific plan. For anyone with ongoing medication needs, confirming that required drugs are on the formulary — and at which tier — is a critical step in plan selection. A lower premium plan can become a higher-cost plan if key medications are off-formulary."],
  [33,"Coordinate","/koʊˈɔːrdɪneɪt/","noun","The process used when an individual is covered by more than one health insurance plan to determine which plan pays first and how much each plan contributes. The primary plan pays first; the secondary plan may cover some or all of the remaining cost.","field","Coordination of benefits prevents double-payment and defines each plan's responsibility. For dependents covered under two working parents' plans, or for individuals with both employer and Medicare coverage, understanding which plan is primary — and how the secondary fills gaps — can significantly reduce out-of-pocket cost.","To manage the payment responsibilities between two or more health insurance plans covering the same individual, establishing which plan pays first and how the secondary plan responds to remaining costs. Coordinating benefits prevents overpayment and maximizes what each plan contributes.","course","To coordinate benefits is to make two plans work together rather than at cross-purposes. The process requires knowing which plan is primary and how the secondary plan calculates its share. Done well, it reduces out-of-pocket cost; ignored, it leaves money on the table."],
  [34,"Subrogation","/ˌsʌbrəˈɡeɪʃən/","noun","The legal right of an insurer that has paid a claim to pursue recovery from a third party responsible for the loss. After paying the insured, the insurer steps into the insured's shoes and can seek reimbursement from the at-fault party.","course","Subrogation is why an insurer may have a financial interest in a lawsuit or settlement involving the insured. If another party was responsible for the loss, the insurer can recover what it paid. Policyholders who receive settlements may be required to reimburse the insurer for claims previously paid.","To exercise the legal right to recover funds paid on a claim from the third party responsible for causing the loss. An insurer that subrogates steps into the insured's position and pursues the at-fault party for reimbursement.","field","To subrogate is to pursue what was paid out — not from the insured, but from whoever caused the loss. Policyholders entering settlements after an insured event should understand that their insurer may have a right to be reimbursed from those proceeds before they are distributed."],
  [35,"Liability Coverage","/ˌlaɪəˈbɪlɪti ˈkʌvərɪdʒ/","noun","Insurance that protects the policyholder against claims arising from injuries or damage caused to others. It covers legal defense costs and any judgments or settlements up to the policy limit. Common in auto, homeowners, and umbrella policies.","field","Liability coverage is protection against the financial consequences of being at fault. The risk is not just the event — it is the legal and financial exposure that follows. Policy limits that felt adequate at purchase can prove insufficient against a significant judgment."],
  [36,"Errors and Omissions","/ˈɛrərz ænd əˈmɪʃənz/","noun","A form of professional liability insurance that protects individuals and businesses against claims of negligence, mistakes, or failure to perform professional services adequately. Also called professional liability insurance. Common for advisors, consultants, attorneys, and other service professionals.","course","Errors and omissions coverage addresses the professional's specific exposure: not physical harm, but financial harm resulting from advice, service, or failure to act. For anyone providing professional services, it is the protection that stands between a client complaint and a career-altering judgment."],
  [37,"Key Person Insurance","/kiː ˈpɜːrsən ɪnˈʃʊərəns/","noun","Life or disability insurance purchased by a business on the life or earning capacity of an employee whose loss would significantly impact the business financially. The business pays the premiums and is the beneficiary. Proceeds help the business survive or restructure after the loss.","field","Key person insurance quantifies what a specific individual means to the financial health of a business. It is not about sentiment — it is about continuity. The death or disability of a founder, top salesperson, or irreplaceable technical expert can threaten a business's survival; the insurance creates a bridge."],
  [38,"Buy-Sell Agreement Funding","/baɪ-sɛl əˈɡriːmənt ˈfʌndɪŋ/","noun","The use of life or disability insurance to fund a buy-sell agreement between business partners or co-owners. When one owner dies or becomes disabled, the insurance proceeds provide the remaining owners with the capital needed to purchase the departing owner's interest at the agreed price.","course","A buy-sell agreement without funding is a plan without execution. The insurance provides the liquidity to honor the agreement when the triggering event occurs — typically at exactly the moment when the business is under the most stress. Funding the agreement at the right value and keeping it current as the business grows are ongoing responsibilities."],
  [39,"Annuity as Protection Vehicle","/əˈnjuːɪti æz prəˈtɛkʃən ˈviːɪkəl/","noun","The use of an annuity — an insurance contract that converts a lump sum into a guaranteed income stream — as a hedge against longevity risk, the risk of outliving one's assets. A lifetime annuity guarantees income for as long as the annuitant lives, regardless of how long that is.","field","An annuity used as protection is not primarily an investment — it is insurance against outliving your money. The guaranteed income floor it provides changes the planning calculus for retirement: when core expenses are covered by guaranteed income, the rest of the portfolio can be managed with greater flexibility."],
  [40,"Moral Hazard","/ˈmɒrəl ˈhæzərd/","noun","The tendency for behavior to change in ways that increase risk when the consequences of that risk are shifted to another party — such as an insurer. Insurance, by reducing the financial cost of a loss to the insured, can inadvertently reduce the incentive to avoid that loss.","course","Moral hazard is a structural feature of all insurance, not a personal failing. Insurers account for it through deductibles, coinsurance, and policy terms that keep the insured financially engaged in outcomes. Recognizing the dynamic helps explain many features of insurance design that might otherwise seem arbitrary."],
  [41,"Self-Insurance","/sɛlf-ɪnˈʃʊərəns/","noun","The practice of retaining financial risk rather than transferring it to an insurer — setting aside personal reserves to cover potential losses rather than paying premiums. Self-insurance is a deliberate strategy, not simply the absence of coverage.","field","Self-insurance is appropriate when the potential loss is manageable relative to available reserves — and when premium costs exceed the expected value of claims over time. High-deductible health plans paired with HSAs, for example, are a form of partial self-insurance. The decision requires honest assessment of both the risk and the capacity to absorb it.","To deliberately retain a financial risk by setting aside personal or organizational reserves to cover potential losses, rather than transferring that risk to an insurer through a premium. To self-insure is to make a conscious decision that the cost of coverage exceeds the value of the protection it provides.","course","To self-insure is not to ignore risk — it is to own it. The strategy makes sense when reserves are sufficient, when premium costs are disproportionate to likely claims, or when the risk is frequent enough that paying it directly is more economical than insuring it. The discipline required is the same as any other reserve strategy: the money must actually be set aside."],
  [42,"Group vs Individual Coverage","/ɡruːp vɜːrsəs ˌɪndɪˈvɪdʒuəl ˈkʌvərɪdʒ/","noun","The distinction between insurance obtained through an employer or association as part of a group — where the insurer pools the risk of many participants — and insurance purchased individually, where the applicant is underwritten on their own merits. Group coverage is often less expensive but tied to employment; individual coverage is portable but priced to the individual's risk profile.","course","The shift from group to individual coverage is one of the most consequential transitions in a person's financial life — at job change, retirement, or loss of employer benefits. Individual coverage requires underwriting that group coverage does not, and the cost difference can be significant. Planning for this transition before it arrives is far preferable to responding to it after."]
];
